## Chapter 2 — The American Electoral Process

Opens with **Obama drawing his own Illinois state senate district** in 2001 —
extending it from the South Side to the Gold Coast to reach donors. Ends with the
same story's punchline: the tie on the redistricting commission was broken by a
name drawn from a replica of Lincoln's stovepipe hat, the Democrat's name came
out, Obama got his district. Excellent opening-day anecdote: rules are neither
neutral nor abstract, and politicians work them.

Chapter is organized as **six questions**. This structure is worth stealing
directly for the syllabus.

### 1. Who can vote
History of exclusion and its dismantling: 15th (1870) nullified in practice by
**poll taxes, literacy tests, the White primary**, and reservation-residency
claims against Native voters. Undone by *Smith v. Allwright* (1944), the 24th
Amendment (1964), and the **VRA (1965)** with preclearance over "covered"
jurisdictions. Expanded 1975 (language minorities) and 1982 (effects, not just
intent, standard). The 1975 expansion raised turnout 4–8 points in covered areas
1980–2016, almost entirely from voters of color (Ang).

Current variation: felony disenfranchisement (only DC, ME, VT allow voting while
incarcerated); Florida's 2018 amendment and the 2019 law conditioning restoration
on paying fines/fees — average ~$4,000 owed in an Alabama study — likened to a
poll tax. Primary types: **closed / semi-closed / open / blanket (jungle)**;
blanket struck down in *California Democratic Party v. Jones* (2000) but survives
nonpartisan (LA, WA, CA top-two; AK top-four).

**Registration and ID.** Registration required everywhere except North Dakota.
24 states + DC have **automatic voter registration**; 21 + DC have **same-day
registration**; 36 states have **voter ID laws**.

Effect sizes the book commits to — these are the numbers to hand students:
- AVR: **+2 points registered**; Oregon **+2–3 points turnout** in 2016
- Same-day registration: **+3 points turnout**
- Voter ID: reduces turnout **among those lacking ID** (NC: −1 point primary,
  −3 points general 2016, persisting even after the law was suspended — a
  perception effect), but **no detectable effect on overall turnout** because
  the affected group is small
- Long lines: waiting an hour **cuts next-election turnout by 1 point**
  (Pettigrew), and people of color wait longer

**The counterintuitive throughline of the whole chapter:** both parties assume
easier voting helps Democrats, and the evidence mostly doesn't support it.
Election Day registration in Wisconsin appeared to help *Republicans*. Florida
felon re-enfranchisement, fully realized, nets Democrats ~48,000 votes — real,
but far short of Trump's 113,000-vote margin and nowhere near Rubio's 700,000.
People are *less* likely to support same-day registration when told it helps the
other party (McCarthy; Biggers).

*Shelby County v. Holder* (2013) gutted preclearance by voiding the coverage
formula ("40-year-old facts"). Post-*Shelby*, previously covered counties purged
more registrations (Feder & Miller) — yet studies find Black turnout has **not**
fallen relative to White turnout (Raze; Komisarchik & White). The book holds both
findings at once rather than resolving them.

### 2. How to vote
**Early voting** (47 states + DC), **absentee** (request a ballot), **vote-by-mail**
(ballot sent automatically; 8 states + DC: CA, CO, HI, NV, OR, UT, VT, WA).
Collectively **convenience voting**. Figure 2.1 maps AVR and mail voting by state.

Effects: no-excuse absentee **+3 points** (Leighley & Nagler); early in-person
voting — little evidence of a turnout effect; vote-by-mail **+2 points** in the
CA/UT/WA rollout (Thompson et al.), with early Oregon/Washington effects
(+3–4) fading and concentrated in low-turnout special elections.

**Strategic consequence, and this is the part most syllabi miss:** convenience
voting replaces Election Day with an election *period*, so campaigns must
advertise longer, run **"ballot chasing"** to get mailed ballots returned, and
help with **"ballot cure."** This adds perhaps **25% to campaign cost**.

Partisan-composition effects: essentially none. Early voters are simply stronger
partisans and more interested in politics — i.e. the people who'd vote anyway.
Convenience voting does *not* correct the education/income skew of the electorate.

2020/2024 partisan asymmetry in *use*: 2020 — 57% of Democrats vs. 33% of
Republicans voted by mail; 2024 — 39% vs. 21%. Driven by Trump's attacks even as
other Republicans pushed mail voting.
Pennsylvania **"naked ballots"** (secrecy envelope) — ~1% of mail ballots
invalidated in parts of PA in 2020; 2024 allowed a provisional in-person cure.
Internet voting: no system yet secure enough.

### 3. Who can run
Constitutional floors (House 25/7 years citizen/inhabitant; Senate 30/9;
President 35/14 years resident and **native-born**). State offices vary
(Louisiana vs. Minnesota compared). Values behind them: **competence** (age),
**loyalty** (citizenship), **familiarity** (residency) — with the Woodrow Wilson
1919 stroke as the counterexample on age-as-competence.

Two under-appreciated barriers the book emphasizes more than most texts:
- **Ballot access laws.** Tennessee wants 25 signatures for a U.S. House run;
  Texas wants 1% of the last gubernatorial vote for a party without ballot
  access — **over 83,000 signatures** in 2022. Applies only to new/minor parties.
- **Term limits.** 37 states limit governors, 16 limit legislators; congressional
  term limits struck down in 1995. Term-limited legislators **sponsor fewer
  bills, develop less expertise, and miss more votes**. Note the internal tension
  the book flags: age and residency requirements exist to build competence and
  constituent connection; term limits destroy both.

### 4. When elections are held
Federal terms fixed by the Constitution; general election date (first Tuesday
after first Monday in November) set by federal law in 1845. States vary on
gubernatorial year (presidential, midterm, or odd-year in VA and NJ). **Many
local elections are not concurrent with federal ones** — the hook for Ch. 11's
off-cycle material.

**The permanent campaign.** Trump declared for 2024 in November 2022; DeSantis in
May 2023. Deliberation argument for it (more information, more contact); against
it (little evidence summer TV ads produce votes; deters good candidates; crowds
out governing). Rep. David Jolly quoted calling the fundraising emphasis
"beneath the dignity of the office."

### 5. Where to run — **the reapportionment/redistricting unit**
- **Single-member districts** are the norm; contrast with **at-large** (Maryland
  House of Delegates elects three per district in many districts).
- **Reapportionment** after the decennial census. Post-2020: CO, FL, MT, NC, OR
  each **+1**, TX **+2**; CA, IL, MI, NY, OH, PA, WV each **−1**.
- **"One person, one vote."** *Baker v. Carr* (justiciability), *Gray v. Sanders*
  (1963, Georgia county-unit system), *Wesberry v. Sanders* and *Reynolds v.
  Sims* (1964). Pre-1960s malapportionment: rural areas held state-legislative
  majorities though **two-thirds of Americans lived in urban areas**.
  Constitutionally sanctioned exceptions: **the Senate** (CA ~19.7M per senator
  vs. WY ~293,500) and **the electoral college**. 2016: states may use total
  population, not eligible voters — using eligible voters would weaken Latino
  districts.
  Ansolabehere & Snyder, quoted: the states began the 1960s as the most unequal
  representative bodies in the world and ended the decade with among the
  strictest equality standards. It also **equalized the distribution of state
  spending** — equal votes really did produce more equal power.
- **Racial vote dilution.** *Thornburg v. Gingles* (1986) three preconditions:
  (1) group large and geographically concentrated enough to be a district
  majority, (2) politically cohesive, (3) racially polarized voting by the
  majority. *Shaw v. Reno* (1993) and NC's 160-mile-long 12th District
  (Figure 2.2 shows 1992/1994/1998 shapes) — upheld eventually only when NC
  argued the goal was *partisan* (a safe Democratic seat), not racial.
  Alabama 2021: one majority-minority district of seven with a 25% Black
  electorate; Supreme Court ruled against it and the 2024 map had two.
- **Gerrymandering** mechanics named: **packing, cracking, hijacking**; plus
  incumbent-protection and partisan variants; **majority-minority districts**.
  NC 2024: Republicans won **10 of 14** seats on a small statewide edge.
  But nationally 2024 was near-proportional — R's won ~50.6% of the House vote
  and ~51% of seats, because IL and others gerrymander the other way.
  Partisan gerrymanders held **non-justiciable** federally in 2019 — the fight
  moved to state courts.
- **Five competing definitions of "good representation"** (this list is the best
  single teaching artifact in the chapter — every redistricting reform implicitly
  picks one): geographic communities of interest / largest possible majority gets
  its choice / historically excluded groups get their choice / competitiveness /
  seats proportional to votes. They are mutually incompatible.
- Commissions: 15 states for legislative maps, 10 for congressional (Jan 2025);
  upheld in 2015. Commission and court maps are more competitive and more
  compact — but must be genuinely independent, and don't fully eliminate bias.
  Gerrymandering **does not appear to increase polarization**; residential
  segregation limits how competitive maps can be made.

### 6. Who wins
**Plurality / first-past-the-post** is the norm; majority rule and runoffs are
the exception (LA, GA). **Ranked-choice voting** statewide in ME, DC, AK plus
NYC, Minneapolis, Oakland, SF — and **expressly prohibited in 11 states**.
**Proportional representation** contrasted (NL, DK, SE, IL, CH).
**Duverger's Law** and **strategic voting**. Consequence: only eight independents
have served in Congress in 60 years, only Sanders for a whole career.
Winner-take-all also explains **targeting** — money goes to competitive races and
battleground states because 30% of the vote is worth exactly what 15% is worth.
RCV caveats the book is honest about: it usually produces the same winner as
plurality, voters find it harder to understand, and incompletely ranked ballots
may not count.

**For Discussion:**
1. Arguments for and against term limits?
2. How have electoral rules affected Black Americans' ability to vote and to
   elect representatives of their choosing?
3. How might different rules about who can vote affect how candidates campaign?
4. If a commission drew a map of *only* very competitive districts, what kinds of
   "good representation" might that harm?
5. How does single-member-district plurality disadvantage minor parties and
   independents?

> **Course notes.**
> - Q4 is a ready-made Week 3 debate and it is *sharper* than a generic
>   pro/anti-gerrymandering prompt, because it forces students to defend a
>   definition of representation rather than a party.
> - The six-question structure (who votes / how / who runs / when / where / who
>   wins) maps cleanly onto the first third of the course.
> - Effect sizes above (AVR +2, SDR +3, VBM +2, ID ≈0 overall, hour-long line −1)
>   are the book's own numbers and make a good interpretation exercise: all are
>   small, all are fought over ferociously. That gap *is* a lesson.
> - Table 9.1 (2020 census electoral-vote shifts) belongs with §5 — already
>   actioned in `textbook-alignment.md` by moving Ch. 9's electoral college
>   section to Week 3.

