## Chapter 3 — The Transformation of American Campaigns

Opens on 2024 negativity claims, then **checks them against data** — a move worth
imitating in class. Wesleyan Media Project: in 2024, <10% of TV ads were positive,
~40% contrast, ~50% attack. Compare 2016 (just over half attack), 2012 (over 60%),
2020 (about one-third — a civil Democratic primary plus a pandemic). Meanwhile
71% of Americans told Pew the 2024 campaign was "too negative."
Then the historical deflation: 1828 press on Jackson's mother; 1860 press on
Lincoln ("a horrid looking wretch"). **The perception of unprecedented
negativity is itself the teachable error.**

**Thesis:** rules and reality change abruptly, not gradually, and campaigns
innovate in bursts. Hence **five eras** (Figure 3.1):

| Era | Years | Defining features |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Pre-Democratic | 1788–1824 | Limited mass participation; Democratic-Republican dominance |
| 2. Mass Mobilization | 1828–92 | Mass participation; Democratic Party (1828), Republican Party (1860); professional campaign managers |
| 3. Progressive Era | 1896–1948 | Party decline, participation decline; primaries and secret ballot; R dominance then D after 1932 |
| 4. Candidate Campaigns | 1952–2012 | Competitiveness; money; computers and internet |
| 5. Contemporary | 2016–present | Polarization and geographic sorting; big data and microtargeting; digital outreach |

**Era 1.** Electors chosen by state legislatures; only property-owning White men
voted. Washington's total 1788 campaign expenditure: **two casks of Virginia
spirits for the electors.** 1800 is the turning point — Federalists vs.
Democratic-Republicans, Alien and Sedition Acts, and Aaron Burr's New York
network as the **first recognizable GOTV operation**. Jefferson's team tried to
change Pennsylvania's elector rules mid-cycle and was blocked by the Federalist
state senate — the book calls this the earliest glimpse of battleground-state
strategy. Candidates themselves barely campaigned; surrogates did everything.

**Era 2.** 1828 as the template, born from Jackson's grievance over 1824 (he won
41% and 99 EVs of 261; the House chose Adams after the Clay bargain). Jackson's
operation established three lasting elements: (1) a loyalist inner circle
recruiting county operatives who expected **patronage jobs**; (2) organizing
voters, newly possible because property requirements were falling; (3)
**entertainment** — rallies, torchlit parades, songs, slogans. "Even though many
people criticize modern campaigns for their superficiality, the 1828 election
shows that American campaigns have long emphasized style over substance."
1840 Harrison ("hard cider and log cabin," turning a Democratic insult into a
virtue) hit **80% turnout of those eligible**. Douglas/Lincoln 1858: candidates
campaigned personally **when they expected the race to be close** — a recurring
conditional the book highlights.
1876 Hayes–Tilden: the disputed commission (7-7-1, the independent replaced by a
Republican), then the bargain that **ended Reconstruction**. Preceding it, Black
political power was real: >500,000 Black men voted in the 1870s; Black
registrants were a majority in MS, AL, FL, LA, GA; nearly 2:1 in SC; ~2,000 Black
men held office. All of it dismantled after 1876 by literacy tests, poll taxes,
White primaries — delivering the South to the Democrats for a century.
Also in this era: the **partisan press weakens** (penny press, yellow
journalism, professionalization/impartiality), and **political machines** peak.

**Era 3.** Three features: more personal campaigning, party-weakening reforms and
collapsing turnout, and women's suffrage.
1896 Bryan (18,000 miles, 600+ speeches, 5 million people) vs. McKinley's
**front-porch campaign** — and the money gap: McKinley $3.35M (≈$125.9M today)
with 14,000 surrogate speakers vs. Bryan $300,000 (≈$11.3M). McKinley won by 5.
First election where a nominee used primaries to help win the nomination.
1912: Wilson and Roosevelt both campaigned hard; TR **shot before a Milwaukee
speech and gave the speech anyway** (bullet fractured a rib, lodged near the
lung); Wilson and Taft suspended campaigning out of fairness.
**Progressive reforms:** civil service (undercutting patronage as campaign
payment); the **Australian ballot** (standardized, state-printed, secret —
enabling ticket-splitting and ending party monitoring of voters); and
**primaries** (32 of 48 states by 1917 for all state offices).
**Consequence — and this is the sharpest single fact in the chapter:** turnout
*collapsed*. 1912 turnout ran **20 points below 1896**. Presidential turnout
1932–48 averaged **58%**, against **74%** in era 2. In the South the secret
ballot functioned as a **de facto literacy test**, enforced selectively.
Reforms intended to democratize demobilized. 19th Amendment (1920): the largest
single expansion ever; women initially voted less and similarly to husbands;
Republicans courted them via domestic roles, Democrats as workers.

**Era 4.** Television. Four consequences: (1) fundraising becomes central
(≈25% of House candidates report spending up to half their days raising money);
(2) power shifts from parties to individual candidates; (3) message content
compresses to 30 seconds, competing against commercial ads; (4) **the unit of
analysis shifts from the county to the media market** — a genuinely useful point
for any lab that aggregates geographically.
Primaries become dominant after **McGovern–Fraser**; 40+ states by mid-1970s.
Effects: money needed earlier, organizations built ~18 months out, and messaging
must now distinguish candidates *within* a party — which pushes toward either
ideological extremity or biography (2008 Clinton "experience" vs. Obama "change").
**Polling** professionalizes (Gallup, 1930s–40s; widespread campaign polling from
the 1960s–70s once telephones were ubiquitous).
**Partisan balance shifts:** 1952, 59% D vs. 36% R identification/lean.
1990–2008 average D edge halves to 12 points. By 2024, ~6 points, with several
2024 polls showing a slight *Republican* edge. Figure 3.2 shows group margins
across 1952–56 / 1992–2012 / 2016–24 — the standout being **lower-SES Whites
flipping from strongly Democratic to strongly Republican**, and White southerners
flipping likewise.

**Era 5.** Coalitional change (suburban and college-educated to D; rural and
less-educated Whites to R; Black and Latino voters moving away from D), plus:
- **Big data and analytics** — party files merging state voter files with census
  and commercial marketing data; a profile may include voting history, donations,
  estimated income, race, family structure, mortgage data, magazine
  subscriptions. Campaigns can target *Guns & Ammo* subscribers.
  Honest caveat: it is **unclear whether analytics improved Election Day vote
  estimates** — Clinton 2016 used analytics instead of traditional polling.
- **Field experiments** — defined properly (treatment/control, randomization),
  with the email subject-line example. Obama 2012's commitment was unprecedented
  at the time.
- **Media platforms** — cable, talk radio, podcasts; 2020 digital vs. TV spend:
  Trump $201M digital / $175M TV; Biden $166M / $250M.
- **Misinformation** — three enabling conditions: mainstream media credibility
  collapsed among conservatives; conservative outlets seen as illegitimate by
  liberals; digital platforms plus AI/bots/deepfakes enable precise targeting.
  "The old-school guardians today control neither the dissemination nor the
  content of political information."
- **Sorting** (partisanship aligning with ideology) — defined and distinguished
  from polarization, pushing strategy toward base mobilization.
- **Demography** — Census projects non-Hispanic Whites at 43% by 2060; Latino
  share 19% (2021) → 26.9% (2060). 2024 Republican gains among Latinos; Trump
  targeting Mexican American voters in south Texas; Harris targeting Latinos in
  AZ/NV. Young voters' Democratic lean "all but disappeared in 2024." Rise of
  religious "nones."

**"The Case for Continuity"** — the chapter argues against itself, deliberately:
- Talk radio/cable resemble the 1800s partisan press; canvassing with an iPad is
  still canvassing. Today's campaigns look like **updated nineteenth-century
  campaigns** more than 1980s mass-media campaigns.
- **Campaigns are not getting longer.** Carter announced **691 days** out in 1974.
  Across the four most recent open-seat elections (1992, 2000, 2008, 2016) nominees
  announced an average of **531 days** before the general.
- Cost: 2024 federal elections ≈ **$15.5 billion**, records set — but whether that
  is "relatively" more expensive is unclear.
- Content: campaigns have always favored personality over policy, and **policy
  ambiguity is a venerable tradition** ("make America great again," "build back
  better") because specifics carry costs. Voters do fixate on traits, so it may
  be smart politics.
- Negativity: a well-known study of 1960–2004 found **little systematic change**;
  competitiveness predicts negativity better than era does.

**For Discussion:**
1. In what ways are today's campaigns similar to the second era (1828–92)?
2. In eras 2 and 3, when were candidates more likely to campaign personally, and why?
3. How did television and primaries change campaigns?
4. Has the internet reinvigorated the personal campaigning of earlier eras?

> **Course notes.**
> - This chapter **answers the Ch. 3 supplemental-reading gap in
>   `textbook-alignment.md` partly by itself**: "The Case for Continuity" is the
>   contrarian reading. The era boundaries most worth contesting are 1896 (why not
>   1892 or 1900?) and especially **2016** — the book's own continuity section
>   undercuts it.
> - The turnout collapse after Progressive reform (74% → 58%) is the single best
>   fact in the book for the course's core theme: **a reform sold as
>   democratizing measurably demobilized**. Pairs directly with Ch. 2's finding
>   that modern convenience reforms move turnout only 2–3 points.
> - Q2's conditional — candidates campaign personally when the race is close — is
>   testable against the Week 6 campaign-visits lab.

