## Chapter 4 — Financing Campaigns

Opens on Harris raising **$1B by end of September 2024**, finishing at
**$1.15B vs. Trump's $464M** — and losing. The chapter's spine is that money is
neither trivial nor determinative, and it says so with unusual candor.

### The numbers to teach with
- 2024 federal elections overall: **~$15.5B** (Ch. 3); 2020 presidential cycle
  **$4.5B** (larger, because both parties had competitive primaries)
- Average **House** candidate 2024: **$1.8M** — over 3× the 1980 average
- Average **Senate** general-election candidate 2024: **$13.5M** vs. **$4.2M** in 1980
- Jon Tester (MT-Sen 2024): **$96M raised, ~90% from out of state** — and lost 53–46.
  A clean illustration of nationalized fundraising.
- Where candidate money goes: **52% media**, 11% fundraising itself, 8% admin, 7% salaries
- Party fundraising: $855M combined (1992) → **~$2.3B (2024)**
- Outside groups: $44M (1992) → $490M (2008) → **$4.2B (2024)**
- Outside groups' share of TV ads: **7% (2008) → 35% (2024)**
- **The deflating comparison:** Americans spent nearly as much on *pizza delivery*
  in 2020 (~$14B) as was spent on that year's election. And Ansolabehere/de
  Figueiredo/Snyder's classic question — given what government policy is worth,
  **why is there so *little* money in U.S. politics?**

Figures 4.1–4.4 are all time series (congressional spending, party spending,
independent-group spending, PAC contributions), all in 2024 dollars, sourced to
Campaign Finance Institute and OpenSecrets.

### Rules for donors
Individuals (citizens/permanent residents, **no minimum age**) and interest
groups. Corporations banned from direct giving by the **Tillman Act (1907)**,
unions by **Taft-Hartley (1947)** — hence **PACs**. Disclosure under **FECA
(1971)**: report donors ≥$200 to the **FEC**; ≥$1,000 within 48 hours in the
final days.
**Crucial corrective:** individuals give more than PACs do, contrary to media
impression (Ansolabehere/Snowberg/Snyder). In 2024 PACs were only **21%** of
Democratic House candidates' money and **33%** of Republicans' — less for Senate.

**Table 4.1 — contribution limits, 2025–26 cycle** (the current-cycle table that
replaces the Week 5 lab's hand-entered 2024 figures):

| From ↓ To → | Candidate | National party cmte | State/district/local party | Other cmte (PAC) | Overall |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Individual | $3,500/election | $44,300/yr | $10,000 combined/yr | $5,000 | No limit |
| National party cmte | $5,000* | No limit | No limit | $5,000 | No limit |
| State/district/local party | $5,000 | No limit | No limit | $5,000 | No limit |
| PAC | $5,000 | $15,000 | $5,000 combined/yr | $5,000 | No limit |

\* National party + senatorial campaign committee may give up to **$62,000
combined per campaign** to each Senate candidate.
Individual max per candidate = **$7,000** across primary + general. The aggregate
individual limit was struck down in **McCutcheon (2014)**.
**State variation:** 11 states have **no limits** at all (AL, IN, IA, MS, NE, ND,
OR, PA, TX, UT, VA); MA caps state-legislative giving at $2,000 per two years
while **Ohio allows $15,499.69** — a wonderfully absurd number to put in front of
students.

**Soft money / hard money**, express advocacy, **magic words**, issue advocacy.
Parties raised **$828M in soft money in 2000** (2024 dollars) before **BCRA
(2002)** banned it. The 1998 DCCC anti-Mark Green ad is quoted in full as the
worked example of an "issue" ad that isn't — and Magleby's study found **~80%**
of viewers said its purpose was to persuade them to vote against a candidate.
Fears that BCRA would impoverish the parties proved wrong.

**Table 4.3 — the three independent-group types.** Worth reproducing as a
handout; the distinctions are exactly what students garble:

| | Independent expenditure cmte (**super PAC**) | **527** | **501(c)4/5/6** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Example | Make America Great Again, Inc. | AFT Solidarity | Americans for Constitutional Liberty |
| Contribution limits | Unlimited | Unlimited | Unlimited |
| May donate to candidates / coordinate? | No | No | No |
| Donors disclosed? | **Yes, to FEC** | **Yes, to IRS** | **No** → "dark money" |

Non-disclosure for 501(c)s traces to **NAACP v. Alabama (1958)** — freedom of
association. 501(c)3s barred from campaigning, though the book notes the IRS
recently ruled **churches may endorse candidates to their congregations**.
501(c)4s may campaign if it is not their "primary purpose"; enforcement is
unlikely given IRS capacity and the 2013 targeting controversy.

### Rules for spenders
**The organizing principle: contributions are limited, spending is not.**
*Buckley v. Valeo* (1976) — donating is speech but corruption is a compelling
interest, so contribution limits stand; spending limits fall. Self-financing
unlimited.
**Public funding** survives only where accepted voluntarily. Obama refused it in
2008 (first major-party nominee to do so for the general); **nobody took it in
2020**; in 2024 **only Pence**, and only to retire debt.
State/local systems: New Jersey 2025 gubernatorial (raise $580,000 to qualify;
$2 match per $1; caps $5.5M primary / $12.5M general; spending caps $8.7M /
$18.5M). **Clean elections** in three states — Arizona gubernatorial candidates
need 4,000 contributions of $5+, then are limited to ~$1M primary / $1.6M
general. **Seattle Democracy Vouchers**: $100 per resident to assign.
**Comparative note:** Belgium, France, UK, Spain forbid buying broadcast time
and provide restricted free airtime instead. The absence of that rule is a main
reason U.S. campaigns cost what they do.
**Electioneering communication** (BCRA): ads naming a federal candidate,
broadcast to ≥50,000 people, within **60 days of a general / 30 days of a
primary** — originally not fundable from corporate or union treasuries.
***Citizens United* (2010)** struck that down 5–4 ("associations of persons"),
calling into question similar laws in **24 states**.
**No-coordination rule and how it's bent:** super PACs built before formally
declaring candidacy; footage posted publicly on YouTube for super PACs to reuse.

**Does outside money decide elections? The book's answer is a careful no-but.**
Clinton outspent Trump in 2016 and lost. Obama offset Romney's 3:1 outside-money
advantage with individual contributions. But the 2012 ad ratios are the honest
version: excluding interest-group ads, **53 Romney ads per 100 Obama ads**;
including them, **82 per 100**. Outside spending "kept Romney in the race."

### Strategy implications
- **Citizens.** Donating is participation; donors favor likely winners, using
  polls, coverage, and others' donations as viability signals.
- **Candidates.** DCCC's 2013 orientation recommended **four hours a day** of
  call time. Online fundraising, **ActBlue** vs. **WinRed**, bundlers, donor-list
  sharing. Obama 2012's tested subject line "I will be outspent" beat "If you
  believe in what we're doing" and "Do this for Michelle."
  **Self-financing usually fails:** Perot $63M (1992); Trump ~20% of his own 2016
  funds; Bloomberg + Steyer spent a combined **$1.4B in 2020** — Steyer won no
  primaries, Bloomberg won **American Samoa**. Two reasons offered: self-funders
  are often already-weak candidates, and donor-raised dollars produce more votes
  than self-provided dollars because raising money **signals** viability and
  builds networks (3,000 people giving $1,000 says more than one $3M check).
  **Donors are unrepresentative:** men and White Americans overrepresented, women
  of color especially underrepresented; Republican donors more economically
  conservative than Republican voters, Democratic donors more socially liberal
  than Democratic voters; and donors often live outside the district.
- **Parties and groups.** Dean's **50-state strategy** vs. Emanuel's DCCC
  competitive-races-only strategy in 2006 — the same party money, two
  incompatible allocations, different winners among their own candidates.
  Groups choose an **"access" strategy** (back incumbents of both parties) or a
  **"replacement" strategy** (Club for Growth targeting "RINOs").
  Groups go more negative than candidates; the **1988 Willie Horton ad** (NSPAC,
  not the Bush campaign) is the case study, with the FEC deadlocking 3–3 on
  coordination.

### The reform debate — the chapter's best classroom material
**Case against the status quo:** fundraising burden deters candidates
(especially women, and moderates); money "buys" elections; wealth buys voice
(**fewer than 400 families** gave nearly half of early 2016 presidential money;
business PACs **$6.1B** vs. labor PACs **$264M** in 2023–24); dark money
(**$22.5M of Crossroads GPS's $180M from one unknown donor** in 2012); and
corruption via **joint fundraising committees** (**$3.5B in 2024**, up from
$1.2B in 2016) and convention committees ($132,900/yr per donor).

**Complicating the case — the part most courses skip:**
- "Money buys elections" is **false as stated**: Trump was outspent and won in
  2016 and 2024.
- Incumbents' reelection rates were **above 90% in the early 1980s** when they
  outspent challengers only ~1.5×, and were *slightly lower* in the 2000s when
  they outspent them 2.5–3×. So the fundraising advantage cannot be doing all the
  work.
- Stricter rules don't reliably unseat incumbents; clean elections don't
  reliably increase competition.
- "Money buys votes" has **little supporting evidence**. Limits make any single
  PAC's $5,000 trivial; donations are strategic (given to those who already
  agree). What money more plausibly buys is **involvement and access** — floor
  speeches, committee activity, meetings (Kalla & Broockman's field experiment) —
  and mostly among legislators who already share the donor's view.
- **Experts vs. the public:** surveys of campaign-finance experts find them far
  less likely than voters to endorse "offices are for sale to the highest bidder."
- **Public opinion is not actually behind reform.** Support swings on wording:
  63% for public financing when described favorably (Common Cause 2010), 50%
  (Gallup 2013), 26% (2015), **67% opposed** to government matching (2021).
  Americans answered **5 of 17** campaign-finance knowledge questions correctly;
  ~70% didn't know or were wrong about whether Obama/McCain took public funding.
  In Feb 2024, only **48%** called money in politics "very important" — below
  crime, jobs, economy, immigration, terrorism, education, health care, Social
  Security, Medicare, and taxes. BCRA itself needed the **Enron** scandal to move.
- **Incumbents have no incentive to reform** — they won under the current rules.

**The counterintuitive closer (§ Campaign Finance and the Political System):**
stricter state campaign-finance laws do **not** raise trust in state government.
And on polarization the sign is backwards from the folk theory: **individual and
small donors are more ideologically extreme**, so empowering them may *increase*
polarization, while PACs and parties tend to prefer moderates, so **relaxing PAC
and party limits may reduce it**. Clean-elections programs in Arizona and Maine
appear to produce **more ideologically extreme** candidates. The book's own line:
*"Let's reduce polarization by empowering PACs!" is not a rallying cry you hear
in politics very much.*

**Key terms:** Tillman Act, PACs, FECA, FEC, BCRA, soft money, hard money,
express advocacy, magic words, issue advocacy, independent expenditure
committees, super PACs, 527s, 501(c)s, *Buckley v. Valeo*, public funding, clean
elections systems, *Citizens United*.

**For Discussion:**
1. Why are contributions limited but candidate spending unlimited?
2. Costs and benefits of funding a campaign via (a) private donors, (b) public
   funding, (c) the candidate's own money?
3. How did BCRA and *Citizens United* change what parties and interest groups can do?
4. Arguments for and against the belief that money corrupts politicians?

> **Course notes.**
> - **Week 5 FEC lab:** swap in Table 4.1 (2025–26 limits) as planned. The Ohio
>   $15,499.69 vs. Massachusetts $2,000 contrast and the 11 no-limit states are a
>   free extension — state variation is more vivid than federal limits.
> - Q4 is the Week 5 debate, and §"Complicating the Case for Reform" gives
>   students the harder side, which they will not find on their own.
> - The **small-donors-increase-polarization** finding is the most
>   counterintuitive claim in the book so far and belongs in a debate prompt.
> - The self-financing record (Bloomberg's American Samoa) plus Harris outraising
>   Trump 2.5:1 and losing is the tightest available answer to "does money buy
>   elections" — and it is all in this one chapter.

---

## Chapter 5 — Modern Campaign Strategies

Opens on the Harris advisers' Nov 26 2024 *Pod Save America* post-mortem —
O'Malley Dillon, Fulks, Cutter, Plouffe on the "unprecedented" **107-day
campaign**. Their stated headwinds: **1 in 4 voters rated the economy
positively; 4 in 10 approved of Biden**. Harris had to defend the record,
differentiate without disloyalty, and reintroduce herself against a 2020 primary
record of left-wing positions. Trump's team (Wiles and LaCivita) had to pivot
from a Biden-focused attack to defining an unknown Harris as "extreme," and to
force one question: **who is more likely to create prosperity?**
The chapter then asks — genuinely, not rhetorically — whether any of that
mattered, or whether the electorate simply preferred the early-2020 economy.

### Vote targets — the arithmetic (this is the `vote-targeting/` lab)
Bradshaw's four ideas: the electorate splits into your base / their base /
undecided; those groups are estimable from past results, voter files, and
surveys; you cannot and need not win everyone; therefore direct resources to key
groups **and nowhere else**.

**Table 5.1 — NC gubernatorial turnout**

| Year | Registered | Total votes | Turnout |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2012 | 6,649,188 | 4,505,372 | 67.8% |
| 2016 | 6,918,150 | 4,741,564 | 68.5% |
| 2020 | 7,364,507 | 5,524,804 | 75.0% |
| 2024 | 7,839,911 | 5,699,141 | 72.7% |
| Avg | 7,192,939 | 5,117,720 | 71.0% |

**Table 5.2 — NC gubernatorial votes won**

| Year | Republican | Democrat | Other | R % |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2012 | 2,440,707 | 1,931,580 | — | 54.6% |
| 2016 | **2,298,880** | 2,309,157 | — | 48.8% |
| 2020 | 2,586,605 | 2,834,790 | — | 47.0% |
| 2024 | 2,241,309 | 3,069,496 | 176,392 | 40.1% |
| Avg | 2,391,875 | 2,536,255 | 44,098 | 44.1% |

**The book's worked 2028 estimate**, step by step — reproduce, then break:
registration 8.2M assumed → 75% turnout → **6.15M votes cast** → target 52% =
**3.20M** (they round up deliberately, to avoid hitting the number and still
losing on unexpected turnout) → **base = the 2024 Robinson floor of 40%** =
2.46M → **ceiling = McCrory's 2012 55%** → persuadable = 15% of 6.15M ≈
**922,500** → so the campaign needs **740,000 of 922,500 swing votes = 80%**.
The book immediately flags the honest limit: this gives a *count* of persuadables,
not *which individuals* they are.

**Table 5.3 — county targets** (Republican candidates; average county share of
statewide R vote × 3.20M target)

| County | 2024 Robinson | 2020 Forest | 2016 McCrory | 2012 McCrory | Avg share | 2028 target |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Mecklenburg | 131,361 | 171,123 | 160,501 | 219,927 | 7.12% | 227,840 |
| Wake | 157,912 | 209,183 | 199,356 | 234,584 | 8.36% | 267,520 |
| Guilford | 78,462 | 97,973 | 94,467 | 114,906 | 4.03% | 128,960 |
| Durham | 22,541 | 29,989 | 30,720 | 38,467 | 1.27% | 40,640 |
| Forsyth | 62,320 | 78,475 | 73,589 | 87,499 | 3.15% | 100,800 |
| **Statewide** | 2,241,309 | 2,586,605 | **2,289,880** | 2,440,707 | | 3,200,000 |

> ⚠️ **Erratum confirmed.** Table 5.3's statewide 2016 figure (**2,289,880**)
> contradicts Table 5.2's (**2,298,880**) — a transposed digit. The NC State
> Board of Elections agrees with 2,298,880. The five county rows are internally
> consistent, so the error is confined to the statewide row. This verifies the
> note already in `textbook-alignment.md` §8 and is a genuinely good lab moment:
> students find a published error by cross-checking two adjacent tables.

### Profiling voters
**Probability samples** (400–1,000 completes); declining response rates force
weighting by gender/race/age/education. **Since 2016, Republican response rates
have fallen such that polls consistently overstate Democratic vote share** —
stated plainly, and useful for Week 9.
**Focus groups** (8–20, non-random, deliberately recruited, facilitator-led) —
depth, not generalizability. In use since the mid-1980s, borrowed from business.
**Voter-file scoring**, three models: **turnout propensity score**, **candidate
support score**, **persuadability score** → **microtargeting** (developed
2000–2002), which replaces blanket **voter ID calls**. Needs a very large poll
(~6,000+) because a 500-person poll has too few in any subgroup. Expensive, but
cheaper than statewide voter-ID contacting.
Honest caveat in the endnotes: **mixed evidence on whether persuadability scores
actually work** (Sides & Vavreck, "Obama's Not-So-Big Data").

### Context: incumbency, open seats, district composition
**Retrospective voting** — incumbents run a referendum on conditions; harder to
persuade because opinions are formed. Challengers are less known. **Open-seat**
races are the easiest to craft persuasion strategies for. Harris in 2024 is
characterized as a **"quasi-incumbent"** — neither reelection nor true challenge.
District-composition worked examples: **Collins (ME-Sen 2020)** winning 51–42 in
a state Biden took 53–42 by running on independence rather than party;
**Kelly beating McSally (AZ-Sen 2020)** by painting a senator who voted with
Trump 97% of the time as an extremist — McSally won only 45% of independents;
**Rosen over Brown (NV-Sen 2024)** conceding inflation but blaming corporate
greed. The closing caution: **few candidates substantially outrun their party's
presidential candidate anymore** (Jacobson).

### The decision to run
2024 illustrations: 12 Republicans ran, arguably splitting the center-right and
helping Trump; on the Democratic side **only Dean Phillips challenged Biden**,
which the book calls one of the most consequential developments of the cycle —
Michelle Obama, Oprah, Whitmer, Shapiro, Newsom all sat out, guaranteeing
renomination and delaying recognition of Biden's limitations.
Four factors: **motivation** (including family — Obama's hesitation over time
with his children, per Plouffe), **resources** (money, credentials, time — time
is why working-class candidates are rarer, Carnes), **organization** (2020
Harris/Bennet/Klobuchar reportedly struggled to lock down staff, which itself
generated bad press and hurt fundraising), and **opportunity**.
**Strategic politicians** (Jacobson & Kernell): rarely challenge strong
incumbents, rarely run against the partisan grain or a national tide — a
self-fulfilling prophecy in which incumbents seldom face tough opposition.
Exceptions are real but rare (Cruz vs. Dewhurst 2012 as a career-building
gamble that paid off); some evidence **Republicans run against strategic logic
more often** than Democrats.
**Underrepresentation is a candidate-emergence problem, not a voter problem:**
Lawless & Fox — women win at the same rate as men but run less, across
motivation, credentials, time, and perceived opportunity. For Latinos, a study of
~10,000 statehouse elections 2000–2010 found underrepresentation traced mainly
to **not running**. Newer work finds White Democrats may now be *more* likely to
support candidates of color (Mikkelborg). Recruitment, prior officeholding,
donor lists, and party support are the binding constraints.

### Messaging
**Issue priorities.** Agenda control is the fight — 2024: Trump wanted economy,
gender identity, immigration; Harris wanted abortion, her economic plan, taxes.
"Once enough Americans decided that the election was a referendum on the economy
and the border, Trump (arguably) had won."
**Issue ownership** (Petrocik): R owns national security, taxes, crime; D owns
health care, education, Social Security/Medicare. But candidates **trespass**
deliberately (Clinton 1996 crime bill; Bush 2000 on education) or are forced by
events (George Floyd 2020, which exposed vulnerabilities on *both* sides).
**Issue positions.** **Median voter theorem** (Downs; Hotelling) with Figure 5.1.
Empirically there *is* a general-election advantage to being seen as moderate,
**though it may be diminishing**. Four reasons candidates can't just converge:
prior positions constrain them (Kerry's "$87 billion before I voted against it"
and the windsurfing ad); donors/activists/groups are more extreme than the
median; **ambiguity pays** ("I want to save Social Security") because vague
statements let multiple constituencies read themselves in and give opponents
less to attack; and the theorem assumes voters know their own and candidates'
positions, which they largely don't.
**Whether to attack.** Definitions: negative campaigning, positive campaigning
(advocacy), **contrast advertisements**. Theory: front-runners and incumbents
attack less; challengers and underdogs attack more; early attacks when
impressions are malleable (Perdue vs. Ossoff, GA-Sen 2020). **But large-scale
studies find front-runners and underdogs often don't follow this advice** —
LBJ led throughout 1964 and attacked Goldwater relentlessly.
Outside groups can attack while the candidate stays clean (Swift Boat Veterans,
2004), and **attacks from outside groups may be more credible** than from
opponents. Whether any of it wins votes is unresolved.

### Where and how to campaign
Two models — **mobilize partisans** vs. **persuade independents** — and most
campaigns do both. The mechanism worth teaching: campaigns **price out cost per
persuadable voter** by state/county/precinct, rank-order, and allocate. Research
on 1992–2008 presidential advertising confirms spending tracked
price-per-persuadable-voter. **Sub-presidential campaigns are far less exacting**
because money, data, and talent thin out — which is exactly the Ch. 11 story.
**Effect sizes on tactics — the honest ledger:**
- Face-to-face contact: **minimal persuasive effect** (Kalla & Broockman, 49 field experiments)
- TV ads: **noticeable but ephemeral** shifts in vote choice (Sides/Vavreck/Warshaw; Gerber et al.)
- Digital ads: **minimal** effect on vote choice (Coppock/Green/Porter)
- On *turnout*: TV/radio/digital may help a little; **in-person contact is
  consistently and significantly better** (Green & Gerber)
2020 Republican ground game: RNC + Trump campaign, **2.6M volunteers, 182M
attempted contacts (5× 2016), ~174,000 new GOP registrants**, credited with
neutralizing Democratic registration advantages in FL and NC.

### Strategists
No "typical" campaign; titles (campaign manager, chief strategist, political
director) don't map to fixed authority. Routes in: advertising/PR (Ailes),
journalism/speechwriting (Moyers, Shrum, Rhodes, Shine), polling (Conway,
Anzalone, Teeter), direct mail (Rove), and now **data analytics** (Mook, Plouffe)
and **digital** (Parscale). Wiles's and O'Malley Dillon's career paths are traced
in full — note O'Malley Dillon's **field** background as characteristic of
Democratic consultants.
Theodore White's *The Making of the President 1960* is credited with creating the
cult of the strategist.
**The deflation:** "we know very little about their actual impact." What evidence
exists suggests **hiring consultants is no panacea** (Medvic). Political
scientists are less willing than practitioners to credit strategic genius —
because rules and reality constrain outcomes.

### Evaluating strategy against the four standards (the chapter's own close)
- **Free choice:** campaigns simplify the choice to their advantage; no
  systematic exploration of the full option space. Not deception — framing.
- **Deliberation:** campaigns supply information but "it would be a stretch" to
  say they promote deliberation.
- **Free speech:** the most positive verdict — digital platforms re-empowered
  individual voices; arguably "more speech than ever."
- **Equality:** the sharpest point in the chapter — **"Intrinsic to campaign
  strategy is the notion that some citizens are more valuable than others."**
  Targeted voters get more information than everyone else, which cuts against
  both equality and deliberation.

**For Discussion:**
1. Do campaigns try to convince *all* voters? Why or why not?
2. What factors influence whether a candidate runs?
3. Why are challengers likelier than incumbents to run negative ads?
4. How has microtargeting changed campaigns?

> **Course notes.**
> - Tables 5.1–5.3 are the spine of the `vote-targeting/` lab (now Week 4).
>   The book's 2028 walkthrough is fully reproducible; re-running it with 2024
>   turnout is the natural extension, and the **Table 5.3 statewide erratum**
>   gives students a real published mistake to catch.
> - Q1 is very nearly the equality argument verbatim — pair it with the
>   conclusion's "some citizens are more valuable than others" line for a strong
>   Week 4 debate on **political equality vs. campaign efficiency**.
> - The tactics ledger (face-to-face barely persuades but reliably mobilizes; TV
>   persuades briefly; digital barely does either) is the single most useful
>   table-of-findings in the book for correcting student priors, and it sets up
>   the Table 12.1 cost-per-voter lab (`gotv/`).
> - The post-2016 **Republican survey non-response** point belongs in Week 9.

