## Chapter 6 — Political Parties

Opens on **July 2024 and the removal of Biden** — Pelosi's "It's up to the
president to decide if he is going to run," said 15 months after he announced and
4 months after he had clinched the pledged delegates; Kaine's "I have complete
confidence that Joe Biden will do the patriotic thing." The book's read: **not a
coup, but genuinely unorthodox** — the first candidate switch without elections
since the post-1968 reforms, after millions had been spent on ads promoting
Biden. Pelosi and others wanted an open competition among delegates; instead
nearly every alternative endorsed Harris immediately and she had the delegates
**one day** after Biden withdrew. The lesson drawn: **nominees are still formally
chosen by delegates**, usually responsive to voters but not always — Democratic
leaders also coalesced behind Biden to stop Sanders in 2020, while Trump won in
2016 *against* insiders. "A political party's first priority is to win
elections," and Democrats concluded — "a bit late" — that Biden wasn't the best
option.

**V. O. Key's three parts** (the chapter's organizing device):
**party-in-the-electorate** / **party-as-organization** / **party-in-government**,
each partly independent of the others. Plus the modern extension: parties as
**"multilayered coalitions"** and **"extended party networks"** sharing mailing
lists across candidates, allied groups, and ideological media.
Nice teaching hook: **who is in charge of a party?** The book poses it as a
genuine question with photos of Maddow/Hayes, Obama, Schumer/Jeffries, and DNC
chair Ken Martin, and doesn't answer it. Party membership is equally fuzzy —
some states have no registration at all, and self-described independents mostly
vote consistently for one party (Sanders as the elected example).

**Why parties exist.** Washington warned of "the baneful effects of the spirit of
party," and parties formed anyway — **politicians organized before voters called
themselves partisans**. Aldrich: parties serve politicians who need durable
legislative majorities, a recognizable brand, and persuasion/mobilization
infrastructure. Five democratic functions: aggregate and articulate interests;
organize coalitions; coordinate elections and mobilize voters; coordinate the
legislative process; facilitate collective action.

**The two parties, by the numbers.**
- Since the 1920s the two parties have held **≥95% of House and Senate seats**
  and **≥90% of electoral votes**.
- **Asymmetry** (Grossmann & Hopkins, the book's own author): **74% of
  Republicans identify as conservative** vs. only **61% of Democrats as liberal**.
  Republicans frame politics as a left–right ideological battle; Democrats as
  group competition between the rich and the vulnerable. Consequence:
  **Republicans usually need more independents; Democrats need more moderates**
  because their ideological base is smaller. Figure 6.1 shows donors are most
  ideological, then activists, then ordinary identifiers — in both parties.
- Coalitions: Democrats win big margins among smaller groups (Black voters, Jews,
  advanced-degree holders); Republicans win smaller margins among larger groups
  (White voters, married people). Americans **overestimate** how stereotypical
  each party's composition is, especially for the party they dislike (Ahler & Sood).
- **The diploma divide is the biggest recent change**; in 2024 **racial divides
  between the parties actually narrowed** as Republicans gained Hispanic, Asian,
  Arab, and Native American voters.
- **Competitiveness:** D House majority 1955–94; R for all but four years
  1995–2018. Since the 1990s margins average **8 points in the House, 7 in the
  Senate** — Lee's "insecure majorities," which **incentivize campaigning over
  lawmaking**. Since the 1930s neither party has had more than two consecutive
  presidents. The most recent "landslide" was **Obama by 7**, against LBJ's 23
  (1964), Nixon's 23 (1972), Reagan's 18 (1984). **No party has won the
  presidential popular vote by more than 10 points since 1984**; five of the last
  nine produced a winner without a popular majority, two without a plurality.
  The book's line: *"Democrats and Republicans have been fighting between the
  45-yard-lines for four decades."*

**Why only two.** Rules first: single-member districts, plurality, and
winner-take-all electoral votes (except NE and ME). Third-party high-water marks
listed: TR 1912 (six states), Thurmond 1948 and Wallace 1968 (South only), **Perot
1992 — 19% of the popular vote and zero electoral votes**, Nader 2.7% (2000),
Johnson 3.3% (2016), nothing above ~1% in 2020 or 2024. Ballot access, polarization,
and media/debate exclusion compound it. But third parties **do** move agendas —
and that is precisely why they die: Clinton absorbed Perot's deficit issue, and
Perot's 1996 run collapsed. Green + Libertarian have elected **300+ officeholders**,
almost all local.

**What parties do in campaigns.**
- *In the electorate:* the partisan complexion of a district is a fixed reality;
  advantaged candidates hug the party, disadvantaged ones distance themselves.
  **Geographic polarization:** only about **3 in 10** of the average American's
  interactions are expected to be with someone from the other party, and **10% of
  Democrats rarely encounter a nearby Republican** (Brown & Enos, 180M voters).
- *As organization:* recruitment (harder than it looks — **31% of 2024 state
  legislative districts had no major-party competition**), discouraging
  retirements, money, and GOTV. **Party contact has collapsed:** 31% of citizens
  contacted in 1982 → 19% in 1990 → **≥40% from 2002–2012** → **only 21% in 2024**.
  Figure 6.2: since 2004 party spending shifted from **coordinated** to
  **independent** expenditures.
  Parties also **signal** — target lists steer leadership PACs, allied groups,
  and super PACs.
  The **purity vs. electability** conflict, with **Nebraska's 2nd district** as a
  four-cycle case study: moderate Ashford wins 2014, loses by 1 in 2016; Eastman
  beats him in the 2018 primary over party objections and loses by 1; loses again
  in 2020 *while Biden carries the district*; Vargas loses 2022 and 2024, again
  while the district goes Democratic at the top. A clean, repeated natural
  experiment in nominee choice.
  Also: **county party chairs think candidates of color are less likely to win
  general elections and recruit them less often** (Doherty/Dowling/Miller) —
  connects directly to Ch. 5's candidate-emergence material.
- *In government:* candidates are tied to their party's leaders and the
  president's approval; majority status brings agenda control, access-oriented
  donations, and legislative timing.

**Constraints.** Primary rules (open vs. closed; states usually force conformity);
California's blanket primary struck down in 2000, replaced by **top-two in 2010**
— which "transforms primary elections from internal party elections to two-step
general elections," and advantages the party that **fields fewer candidates**.
BCRA's soft-money ban redirected rather than removed money. **Parties retain one
real privilege: unlike super PACs, they may coordinate with candidates.**
Redistricting; party ID stability; issue ownership; the economy (2024: perceptions
worse than indicators, and voters "credit themselves for their pay raises and
blame the governing party for higher prices"); and **exposure** — a party holding
many seats has few left to win, while a party that just lost badly can gain a lot
on a small swing (2010's +63 followed the 2006/2008 losses).

**Are parties in decline?** The book says mostly no, and separates the claims:
- **In government: definitely not** — more unified over 40 years, more partisan
  voting in legislatures and courts.
- **In the electorate: no** — "independents" are mostly **closet partisans**
  (Klar & Krupnikov); **only 1 in 10 is a pure independent**; party ID is becoming
  *more* important in congressional and presidential elections.
- **As organization: this is where the case is real** — machines died after the
  secret ballot; candidate-centered elections mean **parties serve rather than
  control candidates**; direct primaries removed nominee control. The **"invisible
  primary"** and *The Party Decides* still predict most nominations, but 2008 (both
  parties) and 2016 (Trump) are genuine failures to coordinate.
  Schlozman & Rosenfeld's **"hollow parties"**: Republicans captured by activists
  and unable to marginalize unqualified candidates; Democrats unable to coalesce
  around an agenda.
- **The definitional move worth teaching:** the narrower the definition of
  "party" (official committees only), the stronger the decline story; the broader
  it is (the extended network), the weaker.

**Evolution and polarization.** Southern realignment via racial issues
(Carmines & Stimson); **sorting** (Levendusky); religious sorting (Catholics
split, White evangelicals to R, the growing nonreligious to D). Trump's 2020/24
gains among Black and Latino voters are read as **strengthening** ideological
sorting, not weakening it — conservative voters of color moving to the
conservative party (Fraga/Velez/West).
**Abortion as the model case:** at *Roe* (1973) both parties were nearly evenly
split pro-choice/pro-life; by *Dobbs* (2022) pro-choice Republicans and pro-life
Democrats had become extremely rare.
**Elite polarization** exceeds mass polarization — "this hardly means that the
United States is a nation of extremists."
**Negative partisanship** (Abramowitz & Webster) — disliking the other side more,
without liking your own more. The best classroom example: right before the 2024
election Democrats rated the economy well and Republicans badly; **one week
later, after Trump was declared the winner, the pattern reversed — with no
underlying economic change.**
**Nationalization:** Figure 6.3 — split-result seats/states ran ~20% in 1948,
30–50% in the 1960s–80s, and have been **below 10% since 2016**.

**Evaluating parties against the four standards:** informed choice — party ID is
a serviceable heuristic, but citizens often **change their own preferences to
match a trusted party leader** (Lenz) and will share information they know to be
false if it helps their side (Kim et al.). Free choice — real two-party
competition beats one-party systems, but the majors entrench themselves and
sometimes collude to exclude third parties; **"personalist" parties tied to one
leader produce less experienced officials and enable democratic backsliding**.
Deliberation — competition clarifies differences, but parties narrow the agenda
to what helps them win (2024 transgender messaging is the example given, on an
issue "not considered important by much of the electorate").
And the fair-minded close: *"In the 1950s, many political scientists and
activists criticized the parties for not offering clear alternatives to voters;
now that they do, we complain of polarization."*

**Key terms:** political parties, party-in-the-electorate, party-as-organization,
party-in-government, coordinated expenditures, party identification, negative
partisanship.

**For Discussion:**
1. Would elections improve if party organizations were *more* involved in
   selecting nominees and informing voters — or is candidate-run campaigning better?
2. Distinct party positions: good (voters can tell them apart) or bad (too
   extreme and rigid to compete for moderates)?
3. Should parties maximize seats by running less loyal candidates where they are
   outnumbered, or back only platform-adherent candidates?
4. How have parties' roles changed since the 1960s — gained or lost influence?

> **Course notes.**
> - **Q1 and Q2 are both strong debate prompts**, and Q2 in particular is the
>   book's own version of the polarization argument — better than an outside
>   source because students have the chapter's evidence on both sides.
> - The **Nebraska 2nd** sequence is the most teachable single case in the
>   chapter: four cycles, same district, repeated nominee-choice trade-off, with
>   presidential results as the control.
> - **Party contact falling to 21% in 2024** and **31% of state legislative
>   districts uncontested** are two data points that pair naturally with a
>   contested-elections lab, and they connect Ch. 6 to Ch. 11.
> - The one-week economy-perception reversal after the 2024 call is the cleanest
>   available demonstration of motivated reasoning for a Week 13 discussion.

---

## Chapter 7 — Interest Groups

Opens on the **post-2024 Democratic blame game over "the groups"** — Ezra Klein
blaming "a culture in which nobody is saying no to the groups," with Harris's
2019 ACLU questionnaire answer on gender-reassignment surgery for detainees
replayed endlessly in Trump ads; and the counter from the left (Waleed Shahid)
blaming corporate interests, lobbyists, and consultants, plus the Future Forward
super PAC pulling her away from progressive positions. The framing question is
sharp: **why would a group like the ACLU, which spent almost nothing on the
campaign, matter to the outcome — while a group like Future Forward, which
represents nobody, matters too?**

**Definition and types.** An interest group acts toward shared policy goals but
**does not run its own candidates**, pursues narrower goals than parties, and is
not necessarily aligned with one party. Types: **business** (largest sector —
3,000+ corporations and trade associations with DC offices), **professional
associations**, **labor unions**, **public interest groups**, **single-issue
groups**. Public-interest and single-issue groups are **more likely to
participate in elections but less numerous and less resourced** than economic
groups (Walker). Standing inequality: older Americans are better represented
than younger; corporate interests better than poor Americans.

**Why so many — four causes:** (1) **government expansion** creates lobbying
incentives; (2) **organizational maintenance improved** — direct mail, then
email/social/web, plus rich patrons and foundations, so groups no longer need
members; (3) **social movements professionalized into sectors**, with a
call-and-response dynamic worth naming in class — Brookings → Heritage →
Center for American Progress → American Action Network; Skocpol's shift **from
membership to management**; (4) **tax and campaign finance rules reward
proliferation of affiliated entities** — EMILY's List (PAC + 527s), Center for
American Progress (501(c)3 + (c)4), NRA (501(c)4 + PAC + aligned super PAC).
Disclosure rules also incentivize **flattering shell names** — the California
auto-insurance initiative funded by insurers calling itself "Californians for
Fair Auto Insurance Rates" and inventing a "California Senior Advocates League."

**The money picture.**
- Figure 7.1: contributions have been roughly flat (~$350M in 1998, peaking just
  over $500M in 2016, just under $500M in 2024) while **direct spending exploded
  from near zero in 1998 to $1.5B in 2016 to $3.8B in 2024**. In **2012
  independent expenditures exceeded total PAC contributions for the first time**.
- 2024 share of large federal contributions: **business 62.6%, ideological groups
  24.6%, labor 10.5%**.
- Figure 7.2: **finance/insurance/real estate is the largest sector**; oil/gas and
  building materials lean R; entertainment and publishing lean D; unions
  out-support Democrats more than any industry sector supports either party.
  Most corporate *and* labor PACs give overwhelmingly to **incumbents** — which
  is why direct spending matters more in close races.
- **Outside groups outspent the candidates themselves in 50 congressional races
  in 2024** — "candidates now have less control over their messages in the most
  competitive congressional races."
- Group ads **go negative far more often** than candidate ads (groups have less
  reputation to protect) and are **more issue-focused**; candidates prefer
  biography.

**Ballot initiatives — the section that fills the Ch. 7 reading gap.**
In initiative campaigns **interest groups replace candidates as the main actors**.
Signature gathering is itself a paid industry (California: millions before the
real campaign starts). The **six-Californias 2014** proposal — a wealthy
technology businessman, negligible public support, experts saying voters lacked
authority, kept off only because too many signatures were invalid — is a perfect
illustration that **money alone can nearly put anything on a ballot**.
**Endorsements as information shortcuts:** Lupia's classic California insurance
study — knowing whether insurers, trial lawyers, or consumer activists backed a
measure let low-information voters **vote as if fully informed**. The caveat
(Broockman/Kaufman/Lenz): voters often don't know the groups well enough for the
cue to work.
**Asymmetry worth teaching: group spending is more effective at defeating
initiatives than passing them** (Stratmann; Dyck & Pearson-Merkowitz on status
quo bias).
Post-*Dobbs* abortion measures are the running example — including **Kansas
rejecting a restrictive amendment two months after the decision**.

**Why many groups avoid campaigns.** Most lobbying businesses never form PACs
(**Apple and Berkshire Hathaway have none**); every major industry spends more on
lobbying and charity than on campaigns (Drutman). The reason is a genuine
difference in goals: **groups want policy, not power**. They can lobby whoever
wins; visible partisanship costs them access and their apolitical-expert image;
and their issues (a narrow tax exemption, a small appropriation) never appear in
campaigns anyway. **Consequence the book draws out: the groups most visible in
elections are the least representative of interest groups overall** — more
partisan, more ideological.

**Influence.** Groups face the same limits as candidates and parties — money
matters most where candidates are unknown, spending is lopsided, and the race is
close. Their one edge: **you cannot vote against them**, so they need not protect
a reputation for accuracy. Since 2012 they have aired more ads than parties —
**one in four ads in 2020**. 2024 saw spikes in **dark money** and **hybrid PACs
(Carey Committees)**, which spend unlimited *and* donate limited amounts.
Evidence that does exist: super PACs **extended Santorum's viability** in the 2012
GOP primary and **weakened the influence of early-state wins**
(Christenson & Smidt); experimental exposure to 2012 super PAC ads **lowered
evaluations of Romney**; a **precinct-randomized field experiment in Oregon**
found mailers moved initiative support in the sponsoring group's direction
(Rogers & Middleton).

**Can groups deliver votes?** Only if members perceive a common fate, believe the
group represents them, and vote together. Endorsements matter most in **local
elections**, where voters may know their neighborhood association better than the
candidates. But **leaders are more ideological and more partisan than members**,
so groups routinely overstate what they can deliver. The extended union example
is the best evidence: labor remains a top Democratic donor and volunteer network,
but membership is declining and is increasingly **public-sector**; Biden courted
industrial unions in every campaign and Democrats still failed to regain those
voters, who **agree with Republicans on cultural issues** — and union messaging
"faltered even among many union members."

**Can groups change the campaign agenda? Mostly no.** The League of Conservation
Voters' "dirty dozen" is the illustration of trying. But as group activity has
shifted from single-issue organizations toward **ideological/partisan
money-funnels using the same consultants, focus groups, and survey data**, their
ads have become indistinguishable from candidate ads. **"There is little evidence
that candidates follow interest groups' lead."** Candidates respond to opponents,
not to groups. Republican groups match their candidates' messaging more closely
than Democratic ones do (Franz/Fowler/Ridout). Hence the closing irony:
**"few interest groups are acting at cross-purposes with the parties and
candidates they support… That makes 'the groups' a convenient target when
candidates lose"** — which answers the chapter's opening question.

**Campaigns within broader group strategy — the education-policy case.**
The **2017 Los Angeles school board election** drew **more than $15 million** in a
local race: pro-reform funders (Bloomberg, Reed Hastings, Walmart/Gap/Apple
heirs — **most not living in Los Angeles**) versus teachers' unions, who tied a
reform candidate to Trump and DeVos. Reformers won their first board majority.
Groups **parachute into whatever level of government offers an opening** — school
board, mayor, state initiative (Washington's 2012 charter measure), Congress.
**The irony the book names explicitly: both sides claim to be fighting "special
interests" while doing the identical thing.**

**Evaluation.** Criticisms: selfish agendas, unaccountable deception,
overrepresentation of the wealthy (Schlozman/Brady/Verba), and — post-*Citizens
United* — functioning as vehicles for parties and rich donors, sometimes
undisclosed. Defenses: they serve **free speech**, add non-candidate voices to
**free choice**, and add to **deliberation** even when they deceive. And the
honest complication: Hillary Clinton was **booed by liberal bloggers in 2007** for
saying lobbyists "represent real Americans," citing nurses' and teachers'
associations — "But, sometimes, that is true."
The distinctive worry the chapter ends on: the damage **may not show up in
campaigns at all**, but in policy shaped to court donors — the 2014 Sheldon
Adelson gathering in Las Vegas, where 2016 hopefuls carefully emphasized military
support for Israel and trade with China.

**Key terms:** interest group, constituencies, ballot initiative campaigns,
campaign agenda.

**For Discussion:**
1. Would elections improve if groups left campaigning to candidates?
2. If you worked for a group, would you donate, mobilize members, or advertise
   directly — and what are the trade-offs?
3. Should groups act as campaign adjuncts, or focus on their own issues and
   sympathetic voters?
4. Is it better for voters to hear only from candidates, or do groups force
   clearer positions and better information?

> **Course notes.**
> - **This chapter closes the Ch. 7 supplemental-reading gap in
>   `textbook-alignment.md` on its own terms.** Ballot-initiative spending was the
>   angle I wanted, and the chapter supplies Lupia's endorsement-shortcut finding,
>   the defeat-vs-pass asymmetry, the six-Californias stunt, and the post-*Dobbs*
>   wave. It also connects forward to Ch. 11 exactly as hoped.
> - **The LA school board race ($15M, out-of-town funders, local office) is the
>   single best case in the book for a state-and-local lab** and would pair well
>   with the Week 12 material.
> - Q4 is a clean debate on **deliberation**; Q1 on **free choice vs. equality**.
> - Figure 7.1's divergence — flat contributions, 8× growth in direct spending —
>   is a good small interpretation exercise: the money did not grow so much as
>   **change legal form**, which is a rules story, not a money story.

