## Chapter 8 — Media

Opens on **the Joe Rogan episode that didn't happen**. Trump taped three hours in
Austin ten days out; Harris's team offered one hour in Washington, Rogan refused
to travel or shorten, and the collapse itself became a story confirming a
"shielded vs. unfiltered" narrative. Harris instead did *Call Her Daddy*,
*Club Shay Shay*, *Pod Save America* — outlets **demographically overlapping her
existing supporters**. Rogan endorsed Trump on Nov 4, having said in January 2020
he'd "probably vote for Bernie Sanders." Rogan's audience: **~11M listeners, 14M
Spotify followers, ~75% male, 56% aged 18–34** — and young men swung hard to
Trump in 2024. The book is careful: *"That does not mean Rogan made the
difference."*
Three themes it extracts, which organize the chapter: **new formats reach people
who catch news only incidentally**; **traditional outlets still frame the story
even when the substance came from a podcast**; and **media and candidates have
incompatible incentives** — Rogan wants length, spontaneity, tangents; candidates
want discipline, low risk, targeting.

### Who the news media are — the audience numbers
- **Print newspapers: 20.9M daily copies (2022) vs. a 63.3M peak in 1984.**
  Digital subscribers: NYT >11M, WSJ 3.8M, WaPo 2.5M. Audience concentrating into
  a few *national* papers. Fewer reporters → less political news → **measurably
  reduced political knowledge and participation** (Hayes & Lawless).
- **Television is still the most popular source.** 69% sometimes watch local TV
  news, 40% often; 393 stations, ~10 hours/week of live news each. Local news is
  light on campaigns (sports and weather dominate) but remains the **most trusted**.
- Network evening news: **19.3M (2025), down from 40M+ in 1980**. Morning shows
  7.3M. Sunday shows 9.3M/week; newsmagazines 7.8M.
  **The average presidential candidate sound bite on TV news is nine seconds.**
- Cable: prime time ~4.35M (2024), daytime 2.76M; Fox leads throughout the day.
  Cable reaches **fewer people but more total minutes**.
- Radio/podcasts: ~1,500 commercial news/talk stations, 1,000+ NPR members; NPR
  10.8M/week. **The top political talk hosts in 2024 were all conservative**
  (Hannity, Kilmeade, Travis & Sexton, Loesch). **40% of Americans listen to a
  podcast weekly — double 2019.**
- Social news (2024): **Facebook 33%, YouTube 32%, Instagram 20%, TikTok 17%,
  X 12%, Reddit 8%, Snapchat 5%** — but most political news circulating there,
  especially state/local, **originates with newspapers**, and users often can't
  recall the source.
- Largest news sites average 27M unique monthly visitors — **but the average visit
  lasts two minutes**, versus far longer for TV news. Good number for a lab.
- Misinformation sites in 2016 were real but **concentrated among the most
  conservative audiences, who needed no persuading** (Guess/Nyhan/Reifler).
- Age: under-30s are **half as likely** as over-60s to follow news generally, but
  they mostly see the same stories redistributed socially.
- **Prior's point, which the chapter leans on repeatedly:** given the choice, most
  Americans prefer sports and entertainment. News junkies are stronger partisans.
  So **the media have an incentive to cater to the already-convinced**, unlike
  candidates who must chase the undecided.

### Nationalization — the mechanism, cleanly stated
Local advertising and subscription revenue collapsed → state/local reporting
became unsustainable → audiences chose national outlets → the national business
model strengthened and the local one weakened further. Local outlets now cover
national politics with local angles emphasizing **national personalities and
stakes**.
Result: Americans know less about state and local politics — **even their
governor's name** — and more about national actors, so they vote on views of the
president rather than of their own representatives (Hopkins).
**The natural experiment worth teaching (Moskowitz):** people who live in one
state but inside another state's media market know little about their own
governor and senate candidates — **and are more likely to vote straight-ticket**.

### Government oversight — thin
First Amendment; FCC regulates **broadcast only** (spectrum scarcity rationale),
not cable. The key rule is the **right to equal time** — but news interviews,
on-the-spot events, and **debates are exempt**, which is precisely how debate
organizers **exclude third-party candidates without violating it**. Equal time
also doesn't require *any* meaningful campaign coverage.
Comparative: other democracies have well-funded public media and more
requirements, and therefore **more free time for candidates to speak directly to
voters without media interruption**.
Social platforms self-regulate — the Jan 6 deplatforming decisions were their own
— but **informal government pressure is real** (Covid misinformation requests),
and after 2024 platforms courted Trump, with Zuckerberg pledging to scale back
misinformation policing.

### News as a business, and objectivity
Public-company ownership; Craigslist destroyed classifieds; digital ads pay less;
Facebook and Google offer better local targeting. Responses: **cut staff** (Rocky
Mountain News closed; Seattle Post-Intelligencer went online-only; bureaus
closed) and **raise revenue** (paywalls — which shrink the audience toward the
already-engaged, or **infotainment** for free outlets). Hamilton: nightly news has
increased celebrity/crime coverage and **decreased coverage of important
congressional votes**. Counter-trend: The Upshot, Vox — though **ABC shuttered
FiveThirtyEight**.
**Objectivity is historically contingent**, not natural: the partisan press
persisted into the 1900s (the *Arkansas Democrat-Gazette* and Springfield's *The
Republican* still carry the names); owners wanted broader audiences; the ASNE code
(1922) codified impartiality; **wire services** (telegraph → AP) spread shared
content; Vietnam and Watergate produced investigative scrutiny.
**"False balance"** and the Trump exception — the *Washington Post* catalogued
**30,573 false or misleading claims** across four years, which reporters took as
license for a less evenhanded posture, temporarily **raising Democrats' trust in
the media**. But reporters generally avoid arbitrating, which **incentivizes
candidates to misrepresent**. In 2016 **coverage of Clinton's emails and
foundation exceeded coverage of any Trump scandal**, and voters reported hearing
more about her emails than anything else (Patterson).

### What gets covered
**Which races:** competitive ones (front-runners campaign conservatively and
nothing dramatic happens — with the nice irony that **uncompetitive races might
become competitive if voters learned about challengers**); higher-authority
offices; races where the outlet's audience can actually vote (Snyder &
Strömberg); celebrities; and scandals — Anthony Weiner's 2013 mayoral run and
"Carlos Danger" as the case in point.
**News values:** **novelty** (hence manufactured event streams and announcement-
as-news), **personality** (amateur psychology — Obama unflappable, McCain angry,
Romney out of touch, Clinton guarded, Trump volatile, Biden forgetful),
**conflict** (negative ads get discussed far more than positive ones **even when
positive ads are more numerous** — so candidates can release attack ads on YouTube
and never pay to air them; and intra-party dissent gets amplified), **skepticism**,
and above all **strategy**.
**Horse race journalism** — **42% of 2016 general-election presidential coverage
was horse race vs. 10% policy**; in 2020 on one network, **74% of Biden's coverage
and 35% of Trump's** was horse race. Plus **interpretive journalism** — telling
audiences what is "really going on."
Debates are scored as won/lost; **one experiment found viewers' opinions of a
debate were shaped more by the tone of post-debate coverage than by the
performance itself** (Norton & Goethals). 2024 is the sharp illustration on both
sides: Biden's June debate **cost him the nomination**, while Harris was widely
judged to have won the only Harris–Trump debate **and the polls barely moved**.

### News coverage of polls — a full subsection, directly usable in Week 9
- Polls let reporters attribute concerns to "the public" rather than themselves,
  and supply seemingly scientific progress measures. **Releasing a poll is itself
  a news event.**
- **Coverage disproportionately features polls showing a closer race** (Searles
  et al.) — drama sells.
- Single polls get overplayed; small changes get over-read; hence polling
  averages exist. Uncertainty comes not just from margin of error but from
  **the many discretionary choices pollsters make** (Clinton, "Poll Results Depend
  on Pollster Choices as Much as Voters' Decision").
- **Subgroup reporting is the worst offender** — "Latina women, young White men,
  married college graduates" — because subgroup margins of error are much larger.
- **The single most useful misconception to correct:** a poll showing 51% is *not*
  a 51% chance of winning. And voters may **abandon a preferred candidate** on
  seeing others ahead (bandwagon effect, Farjam).
- **The book's defense of polls is worth keeping:** the alternative is worse —
  unrepresentative one-on-one interviews, or rally-crowd impressions. Polling
  makes it harder to claim a pet theory applies broadly. *"You can often find a
  few voters to support nearly any theory."*

**How news values fueled Trump's 2015 rise:** little support from party leaders,
enormous audience interest; rivals delayed attacking to avoid offending his
supporters; nicknames goaded responses; every gaffe produced another cycle.
"All publicity is good publicity." He got **more coverage than all other
candidates combined**, and voters learned his positions and gave him credit for
stances other candidates shared.

### Is coverage biased?
Outlet-level: newspaper coverage does favor candidates the paper endorses (Kahn &
Kenney); word-usage analyses do classify outlets left and right (Gentzkow &
Shapiro) — **but this may be demand-side (what audiences want) rather than
supply-side.**
System-level: despite most reporters identifying as liberal and news being
produced in Democratic metros, **content studies find little evidence of
consistent directional bias** (D'Alessio & Allen meta-analysis). The conservative
critique has nonetheless reduced confidence in the press and **made it harder for
citizens to learn facts from coverage** (Ladd).
**The bias that is real is horse-race bias — coverage favors whoever is ahead.**
2012: four negative Romney stories per positive one from late August to early
October, reversing after Obama's poor first debate. 2016: **77% of Trump coverage
negative** (easing in the final two weeks as he gained) and **64% of Clinton
coverage negative** (worsening in the final two weeks).

### Algorithms — the section that fills the Ch. 8 gap
The honest finding, and it cuts against the popular story: **Facebook's
algorithmic experiments reducing like-minded content did not change users'
attitudes; disabling resharing had few effects; YouTube's algorithm does not
reliably send users down a "rabbit hole" but directs them to mainstream sources**
(Haroon et al.). *"It is often the users who choose to consume biased
information, not an algorithm that chooses it for them."*
The residual worry the book keeps: algorithms can **reduce who sees a campaign's
message** and may **boost outrage-provoking memes** even without changing views.

### Effects on citizens
Partisan-media-causes-polarization evidence is **mixed**. Most MS NOW viewers
watch some Fox; **online ideological segregation is *less* than face-to-face
segregation** (Gentzkow & Shapiro) — conservatives talk mostly to conservatives
in person but browse a mix. Most Americans have **moderate or mixed media diets**;
the skew comes from a small group of heavy partisan users (Guess).
**The exception, and it's a real one: Fox News.** Studies exploiting **channel
position and geographic availability** for causal leverage find **easier Fox
access increased Republican voting**, while **MS NOW had no comparable effect on
Democratic voting**, partly due to much lower viewership (DellaVigna & Kaplan).
Why persuasion is generally hard: (1) most people don't follow politics; (2) among
those who do, few are undecided, and they are **motivated reasoners** who
interpret news to fit prior views. *"People have an impressive ability to ignore,
rationalize, or argue against information that is contrary to their views."*
**Where media effects are strongest: primaries** — no party cue, unfamiliar
candidates. Coverage fueled Trump and Sanders in 2016 and the
flavor-of-the-month rises of Buttigieg and Harris in 2020 — **and then the
scrutiny that followed reduced their support**.
The three real effects: **agenda setting** (Iyengar & Kinder — what people think
*about*), **priming** (the criteria used to judge candidates — 2024 inflation
coverage priming judgments of Biden and Harris), and **informing**. Not mutually
exclusive.
The structural limit (Prior): more channels means the uninterested can now avoid
news entirely, producing **a divided electorate — an attentive, participating
minority and a tuned-out majority**.

### Evaluation against the standards
- **Free choice:** coverage informs, but it isn't designed to educate — it's
  designed to hold audiences for advertisers, so most campaigns get little
  attention and the covered ones get novelty, drama, and scandal.
- **Equality:** wide reach happens only for big breaking stories; otherwise
  segmentation. **Information inequality tracks and may reinforce economic
  inequality** — lower-income and less-educated people see less political news.
- **Deliberation:** in a large democracy deliberation must be mediated. Media do
  report differences and sometimes referee. Misinformation is a real threat —
  **Pizzagate**, where a man drove from North Carolina and fired a rifle in a DC
  restaurant — but scholars find **traditional coverage still dominates campaign
  information**, and the larger effects come when **candidates themselves promote
  the conspiracy and tie belief to partisanship**.
  Social media as antidote is ambiguous: the **61-million-person Facebook
  experiment** found seeing friends' "I voted" reports slightly raised turnout
  (Bond et al.), but token online support may **reduce** offline involvement
  ("slacktivism").
- **And the accountability the chapter insists on:** given a menu of candidate
  biography, issues, strategy, and horse race, **people choose the horse race** —
  and the heaviest news consumers choose it most (Iyengar/Norpoth/Hahn). *"It is
  not always easy to tell how much blame media outlets and their consumers each
  deserve."*

**Key terms:** news media, sound bite, right to equal time, infotainment, wire
services, news values, horse race journalism, interpretive journalism, pack
journalism, agenda setting, priming.

**For Discussion:**
1. Who is more responsible for weak campaign coverage — reporters or consumers?
2. If you started a state campaign-news site, what would you cover, and would
   audience-building change your coverage?
3. How should candidates choose where to sit for long interviews? Should
   mainstream media demand more access?
4. How should media respond to polarized audiences — pick a niche or speak
   across divides? Should algorithms give users more of what they want?

> **Course notes.**
> - **The Ch. 8 algorithms gap is now filled, and the finding is the opposite of
>   what students expect** — algorithms mostly don't radicalize; users self-select.
>   That inversion is worth a whole discussion, and it pairs with the Wikipedia
>   pageviews lab better than I assumed in `textbook-alignment.md`, because both
>   are about **revealed attention** rather than imposed content.
> - **"News Coverage of Polls" is a ready-made Week 9 reading.** The 51%-is-not-a-
>   51%-chance error, subgroup margins of error, and the closer-races-get-covered
>   finding are all directly checkable against 2024 poll aggregates.
> - Q1 is the strongest debate prompt in the chapter — it forces students off the
>   easy "blame the media" position using the book's own evidence.
> - The **Moskowitz media-market natural experiment** is a genuinely elegant
>   design worth walking through slowly; it is the clearest causal identification
>   in the book so far.

