## Chapter 9 — Presidential Campaigns

Opens on the **final weekend of 2024**: Harris detouring to *SNL* (just under
1.5M live viewers, plus clips) between Charlotte and Detroit, then Allentown,
Scranton, Pittsburgh, Reading, and a Philadelphia closer; Trump ending past
midnight in Grand Rapids. **From August onward neither ticket spent more than a
day or two outside a state essential to its electoral college path**, and trips
outside the battlegrounds were almost always for fundraising or broad-audience
media.
The framing the book wants: Trump won **all seven battlegrounds** while
*Harris's* campaign was arguably the more geographically focused; Trump's events
in New Mexico, New York, and Virginia recall Clinton 2016's contested reaches,
but may have been cheap path-expansion plus **positive coverage for looking bold**.
And the consequence it insists you notice: **voters in 43 states plus DC were
only indirectly targeted.**
The chapter's stated skepticism, up front: campaigns "reinforce citizens'
underlying partisan loyalties and raise the salience of other fundamental
factors" — *"just because certain campaign strategies are correlated with victory
does not mean that they cause victory."*

### Two tasks, in tension
Win the nomination (≈80% of delegates chosen in primaries, caucuses, or state
conventions), then win **270 of 538**. **Five presidents lost the popular vote and
won**: J.Q. Adams (1824), Hayes (1876), Harrison (1888), Bush (2000), Trump
(2016) — with some evidence Kennedy lost it in 1960 (Gaines).
The tension: primary electorates reward positions that general electorates
punish. Bush 2000, Clinton 2008/2016, Biden 2020 deliberately avoided
nomination-stage commitments incompatible with the general — **a risky strategy
that can alienate the base**.

### Nomination
**Invisible primary** — endorsements, money, organization, standing with press and
public: **necessary but not sufficient**. Early debates matter for breakout, which
is why **front-runners increasingly skip them — Biden and Trump both did in 2024.**
**Rules that matter, in four dimensions:** how states structure contests, how
delegates are allocated, how delegates are selected, and **the order of contests**.
- **Caucuses** need committed followers and organization. Iowa's 2020 app
  debacle led Democrats to diminish it; **Iowa Democrats moved to long-run mail
  balloting (Jan 12–Mar 5, 2024) specifically so the result would land late and
  not influence other early states.**
- **Primaries** closed/open/semi-closed. The conventional wisdom is that open
  primaries help moderates — **but recent research finds minimal variation in the
  electorate across primary types** (Sides et al.), and finds primary electorates
  are **not more ideologically extreme than the party overall**.
- **Allocation:** proportional with a **15% threshold** is the Democratic
  requirement; Republicans historically allowed winner-take-all but have shifted
  toward proportional and hybrid systems.
- **Unpledged/unbound delegates ≈ one-fifth** of the total — free agents to be
  persuaded individually. Sanders worked them hard in 2016 and lost them to
  Clinton; the **Unity Reform Commission** then weakened their number and power.
- **Sequence and front-loading.** Early states matter far beyond their delegate
  counts because results signal **viability** and simplify choice. Illustrations:
  seven Republicans in December 2023 → **two (Trump and Haley) after New
  Hampshire**; nearly all of Biden's 2020 opposition quit after South Carolina.
  National parties discourage front-loading (RNC's 2016 preferred calendar; no
  contests before Feb 1; proportional penalty for jumping).
- **The 2024 calendar fight is the best rules-are-strategy case in the book.**
  Democrats moved **South Carolina first**, then Nevada and Michigan, justified as
  diversity but driven by Biden's strength among Black primary voters. New
  Hampshire's **HB73** requires it to go first; it defied the party, held the
  first primary anyway, **kept its full delegation — and Biden was not on the
  ballot.**
**Front-runner vs. long-shot strategy:** front-runners must decide how much to
contest small early states (Clinton 2008 couldn't skip Iowa without inviting
"is she afraid?"); Biden 2020 downplayed early states, was helped by three
different winners in Iowa/NH/NV, and **went all-in on South Carolina**; Trump 2024
was less aggressive in Iowa and won easily as DeSantis, Haley, and Ramaswamy
split the vote.
**Momentum** benefited Carter 1976, Bush 1980, Hart 1984, McCain 2000, Obama
2008, Sanders and Cruz 2016 — **only two of those seven won the nomination.**

### Running mates — four strategic options
Compensate for weakness (Obama–Biden, Bush–Cheney, Trump–Pence on foreign
policy); carry a state or region (Kennedy–Johnson; Clinton–Kaine for Virginia);
appeal to a group (Pence for skeptical conservatives; **Walz for less-educated,
conservative White voters**); or **reinforce** the nominee's image
(Clinton–Gore 1992; **Trump–Vance 2024** for the MAGA base).
Timing: announce weeks out for two media hits, or right before the convention to
maximize the convention — recent practice favors the latter.
**Effect size: at most a point or two in select states** (Devine & Kopko) — small,
but the book notes Democrats still argue **Shapiro over Walz might have been
worth a point in Pennsylvania**.

### Conventions
Post-**McGovern–Fraser** they are "four-day public-relations spectacles." The
architecture is worth teaching because it is formulaic: Mon/Tue = narrative of
the last four years; Wed = the contrast (VP's speech); Thu = the vision (nominee's
speech).
**Convention bump** — two-party trial-heat share 1–7 days after minus 1–7 days
before. **Average since 1972: ~5 points. Average 2008–24: ~2 points.** Figure 9.1
shows the decline; **Clinton's 1992 bump of ~16 points** is the outlier, and
largely an artifact — **Perot dropped out on the convention's last day**, freeing
about a third of likely voters.
Why conventions beat debates: they come **earlier**, when more voters are
uncommitted, and they generate **one-sided favorable coverage** while the opponent
campaigns less.
**2024: essentially no bump for either — under a point** — with Harris's real
surge coming **before** the convention, right after she became the de facto nominee.
**The methodological deflation worth assigning:** Lauderdale & Rivers argue
bumps are largely **poll response bias** — supporters of the other side decline to
answer during the opponent's convention week, and the effect **fades in ~10 days**.
See also Gelman et al., "The Mythical Swing Voter." Consensus: conventions matter
but are **rarely game changers**.

### The electoral college
538 = 435 + 100 + 3 (23rd Amendment). Winner-take-all is **state law, not
constitutional** — **Maine and Nebraska** split by district, and in 2024 **Harris
won NE-2 while Trump won ME-2**.
**Malapportionment:** Wyoming has one elector per ~149,000 adults, California one
per ~566,000. But the counter-argument the book gives equal weight: winner-take-
all creates large-state windfalls — **Trump won 58.0% of electoral votes on 49.8%
of the popular vote in 2024.**
**Partisan bias:** none consistent 1980–2012; significant Republican advantage in
2016 and 2020 (battlegrounds more Republican than the nation; Democratic votes
"inefficiently distributed" in CA/NY/IL). **In 2024 the advantage nearly vanished
— because Republican support grew in places and groups that stayed Democratic
overall, making the *Republican* vote less efficient.** That reversal is a
genuinely good analytic puzzle for students.
**National Popular Vote compact** — takes effect only at 270; the book flags the
**Article I, §10, cl. 3 compact-clause** question. Opinion (Pew 2024): **64%
favor direct election, 35% keep the current system; 80% of Democrats vs. 53% of
Republicans preferring the electoral college.**
**Figure 9.2 (2000–24):** 35 states + DC voted the same way every time; **15 did
not** — AZ, CO, FL, GA, IN, IA, MI, NV, NH, NM, NC, OH, PA, VA, WI. Those 15 =
**189 electoral votes in 2024**; the seven "true" battlegrounds = **93**.
Democrats start with **194 to 155** in base states. The Republican base is the
**"Republican L"** (South + Mountain West).

**Table 9.1 — Electoral vote change from the 2020 census** (the Week 3 lab's
comparison table):

| Safe Democratic | Battleground | Safe Republican |
|---|---|---|
| California −1 | Florida +1 | Montana +1 |
| Colorado +1 | Michigan −1 | Texas +2 |
| Illinois −1 | North Carolina +1 | West Virginia −1 |
| New York −1 | Ohio −1 | |
| Oregon +1 | Pennsylvania −1 | |
| **Overall −1** | **Overall −1** | **Overall +2** |

### Where the money and the candidates actually went — 2024
- **$356M on broadcast TV Aug 8–Nov 5, with 98% in the seven battlegrounds;
  21% in Pennsylvania alone**; plus **$1.5M aimed at NE-2's single elector**.
- **Table 9.2 — top 10 broadcast markets ($ millions, Aug 8–Nov 5, AdImpact):**

| Harris/Walz | $M | Trump/Vance | $M |
|---|---|---|---|
| Philadelphia PA | 19.75 | Atlanta GA | 16.45 |
| Phoenix AZ | 18.17 | Philadelphia PA | 14.32 |
| Atlanta GA | 16.21 | Phoenix AZ | 12.29 |
| Detroit MI | 15.61 | Detroit MI | 10.94 |
| Pittsburgh PA | 10.44 | Charlotte NC | 10.81 |
| Milwaukee WI | 8.29 | Pittsburgh PA | 9.65 |
| Raleigh NC | 6.08 | Raleigh NC | 8.97 |
| Grand Rapids MI | 5.98 | Milwaukee WI | 7.11 |
| Charlotte NC | 5.53 | Harrisburg PA | 5.01 |
| Harrisburg PA | 5.53 | Green Bay WI | 4.48 |

- **Table 9.3 — appearances, top five states (Aug 1–Nov 5, includes running mates):**

| Harris/Walz | | Trump/Vance | |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pennsylvania | 41 | Pennsylvania | 43 |
| Michigan | 31 | Michigan | 33 |
| North Carolina | 27 | Wisconsin | 25 |
| Georgia | 17 | Georgia | 22 |
| Wisconsin | 17 | North Carolina | 18 |
| **Total** | **193** | **Total** | **184** |

  Top five states took **more than two-thirds of all visits**. Separately the book
  reports **Trump 111 distinct events vs. Harris 96**, and **Democrats outspending
  Republicans on broadcast TV $461M to $447M**.
  > ⚠️ **Scope mismatch to watch in the Week 6 lab.** The $356M figure is
  > campaign spending Aug 8–Nov 5; the $461M/$447M figures include party
  > committees. And Table 9.3's 193/184 counts *ticket* appearances in the top
  > five states only, over Aug 1–Nov 5, while the 111/96 counts are *candidate*
  > events. Our AP-tracker count of 324 stops is a fourth universe. **None of
  > these should match, and reconciling why is the lesson** — sources
  > (campaign sites, NYT, Politico, WaPo, AP), date windows, and whether running
  > mates count.
- **Targeting archetypes** worth naming: **offense** (Bush 1988, Clinton 2016),
  **defense** (Clinton 1996), **"threading the needle"** (Gore 2000, Harris 2024),
  and **mixed** (Bush 2000; arguably Trump 2024, with late Minneapolis and
  Albuquerque–Santa Fe buys that never reduced battleground spending).

### Reality: incumbency, war, economy
**Two-thirds of incumbent presidents seeking reelection win**, starting with
bigger leads and holding more of them.
**War** usually hurts — combat deaths sour evaluations (Hibbs) — 1952 Korea and
1968 Vietnam being the cases; rally effects and "October Surprises" are rare.
**Economy is paramount.** Figure 9.3 plots incumbent-party two-party vote share
against **change in real disposable income per capita, Q3 of the prior year to Q3
of the election year**, 1952–2024. Carter won 45% amid shrinking incomes; Reagan
cruised in 1984. **2024's 1.1% change predicted just over 50% for the incumbent
party; Harris got 49.2% — underachieving by about a point.** This is a
ready-made forecasting exercise.
The asymmetric retrospection is striking: **voters did not blame Trump for the
2020 collapse** (blaming the pandemic and shutdowns) but **did blame Biden for
post-2021 inflation** — with **transportation up 34% and food up 24% from 2020 to
2024**.

### The fall campaign
**Message development** — poll- and focus-group-driven; **narrow-casting** lets
campaigns run broad appeals on TV and base appeals via mail, phone, email
(Harris's Puerto Rican–targeted "Somos Más" ad and texts after the "island of
garbage" remark).
**Going negative — and the presidential exception to Ch. 5's rule.** Ch. 5 says
incumbents shouldn't attack because it elevates an unknown opponent; **the book
explicitly notes this does not hold in presidential races**, where the opponent is
known to everyone, so incumbents *must* define them. Clinton, Bush, and Obama
were all more negative in their reelection campaigns.
2024 (Wesleyan, Oct 1–27): **~90% of both candidates' ads were contrast or
attack; ~50% of Trump's and ~35% of Harris's were pure attack** — against 57%
(2016) and 63% (2012), with 2020 unusually positive.
The strategic logic given for each: attitudes toward Trump were fixed (**felony
convictions and an assassination attempt barely moved them**) so persuasion
spending was wasted, while Harris was still definable; Harris needed the race to
be about Trump because the economy and border favored him and **abortion was her
one issue advantage post-*Dobbs***. The postmortem critique: **too much Trump, not
enough positive message.**
**Timing** — no conclusive evidence for any strategy. Trump 2016 back-loaded;
convention bumps suggest early is more movable; voters 2004–24 decided **earlier**
than in 2000; but ad and visit effects **dissipate within days** (Gerber et al.;
Shaw & Gimpel).
**Debates** — goal one is "do no harm"; **boring is good**. Prep: an hour or two
weekly through summer, then all day for three to four days. **2024 is the
anomaly**: Biden's June 27 debate — the earliest in history — ended his
candidacy within about three weeks. Harris was judged to have won Sept 10 **and
the polls barely moved**. Normally debates do little: they come late, partisans
score them for their own side (CNN 2020: **93% of Democrats said Biden won, 73%
of Republicans said Trump won**), and unlike conventions they broadcast **both**
messages, which cancel out. **Post-debate coverage matters more than the debate**
— Gore was judged the winner of the first 2000 debate and, one week later, the
loser.
**Mobilization** — **convenience voting reshapes the endgame**: 69% voted before
Election Day in 2020; **60% in 2024 (29% mail, 31% early in person)**. Figure 9.4
tracks modes 2004–24 (Election Day in-person 79% → 40%). State detail for 2024:
**Arizona 68% early, Georgia 78%, Pennsylvania 45%** — so late GOTV launches are
malpractice. Campaigns monitor early-vote files to re-target non-voters and
recruit those who have voted.
Ground-game lineage: Gore's 72 sleepless hours (2000) → Republican **"72-hour
plan"** (2002, 2004) → Obama 2008/2012 → Trump 2016's half-digital media budget →
Biden 2020's claimed **58 million contacts** → **Harris 2024's claimed 90,000+
volunteers and 3 million doors**.
**Election Day and after** — precinct reports show where turnout is "light" or
"heavy"; lawyers file to keep polls open; and the book's forward-looking claim:
**expanded convenience voting plus polarization make post-election campaigns "a
durable feature."**

### Evaluation
- **Unrepresentative nominees?** The book says the record refutes it — McCain,
  Dole, both Bushes, Romney, both Clintons, Gore, Kerry, Obama, Biden, Harris
  "hardly seem out of the mainstream," consistent with research that primary
  electorates are not more extreme than their parties. **But** Biden's
  uncontested 2024 primary and Harris's selection **without direct voter input**
  cut the other way, and tighter future party control would fuel the critique.
- **Conventions as fluff?** "Undoubtedly justified" as to deliberation — but they
  unify parties, nominee speeches **are** policy-rich, and interest rises afterward.
- **Debates scripted?** Hard to say of 2016/2020/2024. They raise interest,
  favorability, and some knowledge — **though not necessarily about issue
  positions**. Town hall formats impress citizens most.
- **Electoral college.** Defenders: splits are rare; **battlegrounds are actually
  quite representative of the country** (Goux & Hopkins); and battleground
  turnout is higher due to **surge in those states, not demobilization
  elsewhere** (Gimpel/Kaufmann/Pearson-Merkowitz; Lipsitz). Against direct
  election: it would not stop selective campaigning (parties would still chase
  their own concentrations) and would make campaigns **longer and more
  expensive**. Closing line: *"the words perfection and democracy rarely appear
  in the same sentence."*

**Key terms:** battleground/swing states, invisible primary, delegates, caucuses,
primary election, viability, front-loading, front-runners, roll-call vote,
convention bump/bounce, narrow-casting.

**For Discussion:**
1. Why do states move primary dates up? What is it called, and what does it do to
   campaigning?
2. How do conventions today differ from the 1950s?
3. How does the electoral college shape presidential strategy?
4. If you ran a presidential campaign, would you emphasize TV, digital, or ground
   game?

> **Course notes.**
> - **Table 9.1 is the Week 3 accuracy check** for the Huntington–Hill lab, as
>   already planned — and note it is organized by *safe D / battleground / safe R*,
>   which adds a second lesson: the same reapportionment arithmetic has partisan
>   consequences the method itself is blind to.
> - **Tables 9.2 and 9.3 are the Week 6 cross-check**, with the scope-mismatch
>   warning above. The discrepancy is the deliverable, not a problem to fix.
> - **Figure 9.3 is a free forecasting lab**: one predictor (Q3-to-Q3 real
>   disposable income), a published prediction of "just over 50%," and an actual
>   of 49.2%. Students can extend the series and argue about 2028.
> - Q4 is answerable directly from Ch. 5's effect-size ledger plus this chapter's
>   convenience-voting numbers — a good synthesis prompt for late in the term.
> - The **2024 disappearance of the Republican electoral college edge** is the
>   most interesting new fact in the chapter and is not yet anywhere in our
>   materials.

