## Chapter 10 — Congressional Campaigns

Opens on **Bob Casey losing PA-Sen 2024 by 15,000 votes out of 7 million —
0.22 points** — after winning by 17 in 2006 and cruising in 2012 and 2018. Casey
had done everything the old playbook prescribes: moderate reputation, long
pro-life record (shifting post-*Dobbs*), a border-security vote, and an
opponent-lives-in-Connecticut attack. **"But Casey faced a problem he couldn't
really fix: Joe Biden."** McCormick's debate line — *"Sen. Casey stood next to Joe
Biden when he could hardly finish a sentence… and said he's ready to go"* — is
nationalization in one sentence. The symmetry the book draws: **nationalization
elected Casey in 2006 (against an unpopular Bush) and defeated him in 2024.**
The counter-case: **Tammy Baldwin won Wisconsin by ~1 point while Trump carried
it by about the same**, running on deep state roots and Eric Hovde's Laguna Beach
house — *"the exception that proves the rule."*

**The chapter's headline claim (its own Core of the Analysis, first bullet):
"Congressional elections have been nationalized, weakening the incumbency
advantage."** This is the testable claim for Week 11.

**The paradox it sets up immediately:** the incumbency *advantage* is collapsing
while incumbents keep winning — **97% of House incumbents and 88% of Senate
incumbents were reelected in 2024**. Resolution: incumbents win because they sit
in districts their party already carries, not because incumbency itself buys
votes.

### Who runs
Legal barriers are minimal; the filter is strategic and social. **Figure 10.1**:
1946–2024 average reelection rate for incumbents seeking it — **House 93%,
Senate 81%**.
Retirement triggers: scandal (**66 House retirements after the 1992 House banking
scandal**), redistricting (**Tennessee 2021 splitting Nashville into three
districts, each pairing Democratic urban voters with more suburban/rural
Republicans — Jim Cooper saw it and retired; all three elected Republicans in
2022**), and adverse waves (more Republican retirements 2017–20, more Democratic
retirements 2021–22).
**Quality challengers** (prior officeholders) vs. **political amateurs**. The
notable trend: **amateurs are more likely to win today than in previous decades,
which means political experience is worth less than it used to be** (Porter &
Treul). Amateurs increasingly run in open seats rather than only against
incumbents.
Interest groups now substitute for party backing — **Club for Growth** on the
right, **Justice Democrats** on the left (**Ocasio-Cortez over 10-term Joseph
Crowley, 2018**). The Club for Growth's stated logic is worth quoting to students:
*"Why spend a lot of money in the primary and then spend a lot of money again in
the general, when you can operate in districts where you can spend a lot of money
in the primary and spend nothing in the general?"*
**Why Congress is unrepresentative — and the book is explicit that it is a
candidate-supply problem:** those who run are wealthier, more educated, whiter,
more male, and concentrated in business, law, and politics because those careers
supply **fundraising networks**. Lawyers are especially good at raising early
money (Bonica). Women and people of color are underrepresented **in those
network-rich professions** (Thomsen & King).

### Money
2023–24 cycle averages: **House incumbent $3M vs. House challenger $467,000;
Senate incumbent $31.2M vs. Senate challenger $2.8M.** Competitiveness drives
spending — **Klobuchar spent "just" $24M in an easy Minnesota race while Baldwin
spent $52M in comparably sized Wisconsin.**
**No public financing for congressional candidates.** Congressional candidates
lean on PACs far more than presidential ones do: 2024 House Democrats **65%
individual / 21% PAC**; House Republicans **51% / 33%**; Harris and Trump were
almost entirely individual-funded. (PAC limits of $5,000 are trivial at
presidential scale, material at House scale.)
**Out-of-district money as the measure of nationalization:** median **House
incumbent got 42% of individual contributions from outside the district**; median
**Senate incumbent 68%**; **Tester 89% out-of-state**. Donors are motivated by
**partisan control of Congress**, not representation.
**61 House and Senate candidates self-funded $1M+ in 2024.**
Outside money can dwarf the candidates: **Wisconsin 2024 — outside groups spent
almost $120M against the candidates' combined $90M.**

### Nomination
Many primaries are uncontested; ideological challenges are rising but rarely
succeed — **just four House incumbents lost primaries in 2024**. Open-seat
primaries are the competitive ones. Party recruitment programs (**NRCC "Young
Guns," DCCC "Red to Blue"**) supplied 22% of Republican primary candidates in
2010 and 24% of Democratic ones in 2018 — **but ~10% is typical**.
**Two contrasting strategies, and the book gives each a full case:**
- **Maggie Hassan (NH)** — balance: pro-choice, but voted against parts of
  Biden's agenda (minimum wage) and for the Laken Riley Act; ranked among the most
  bipartisan senators; **won by 9 in 2022 while Harris won NH by only 3 in 2024**.
- **Andy Ogles (TN-7)** — no moderation at all: won a gerrymandered
  Nashville-splitting seat in 2022 attacking rivals as "too liberal for
  Tennessee," backed overturning *Roe* **and** *Obergefell*, survived credential
  and campaign-finance scandals, introduced a bill to send campus-protest
  arrestees to six months of community service **in Gaza**, then **won his 2024
  primary by 13 and the general by more than in 2022**.
Why non-moderation persists: safe districts, donor obligations from the primary,
and research showing **voters refuse to punish their own party's more extreme
general-election candidates** (Highton & Stone).

### General election
**Voters know very little:** only **one in five Americans can name both of their
senators**, and only **a third know their House member's name**. So much of a
congressional campaign is simply name introduction — which is why challengers are
so badly disadvantaged.
Message: incumbents run on experience and delivery; challengers contrast, attack,
and use the **"Washington insider" attack** (Amy McGrath's 2020 "swamp turtle"
ad against McConnell — *"Well, they should feel lucky to see me"* — with the
honest note that **two of the coal miners present disavowed the ad**).
**"All politics is local" is now false** and the book says so: national issues
dominate (Johnston, *The Nationalization of Congressional Politics*). Ogles's
own issues page is the exhibit — abortion, abolishing the Department of
Education, gun rights, immigration, with the district mentioned only through
them. Causes: professionalized national party messaging and party-line vote
pressure (Lee); stronger voter party loyalty making cross-party personal appeals
futile; and national issues raise money from out-of-district partisans.
**Communication economics — a genuinely good teaching point:** House candidates
rely least on TV because **ads are sold by media market, not district**, so a
House candidate pays to reach neighboring districts. **New Jersey has no media
market of its own** — its north is in the New York market, its south in
Philadelphia — so NJ candidates must buy two of the most expensive markets in
the country. Hence digital, radio, billboards, yard signs. **Many House races
feature no television advertising at all.**
Social media is thinner than assumed: as of 2023–24, researchers found official
**TikTok accounts for only 7 senators and 34 House members**.
**Earned media** matters most to those least able to get it — local news barely
covers campaigns. Hence stunts like **Ian Golden walking a 450-mile district**
(NY-23, 2018).
Mobilization: congressional candidates free-ride on presidential turnout in
presidential years and must generate it themselves in midterms — **which is why
midterm turnout is lower**.
**Party committees:** protect vulnerable incumbents first (**DCCC "Frontline" —
29 incumbents in 2023–24**), then open seats of departing members, then flip
seats when conditions favor. **NRCC targeted 25 Democrats for the 2025–26 cycle
over the "One Big Beautiful Bill" vote.** Coordinated expenditures are limited
like contributions, so committees rely on **independent expenditures**.
**NM-2 as the recurring case:** 2022 — DCCC spent $2M attacking Herrell, NRCC
$2.3M attacking Vasquez; **Vasquez won by under a point**. 2024 rematch — DCCC
$3.2M, NRCC $1.3M; **Vasquez won again**. And it may run a third time in 2026.

### The incumbency advantage — the core evidence
**Figure 10.2 (1954–2024):** about **2 points in the late 1950s**, rising to
**just under 10 points at its 1986 peak**, then declining to **1.2 points in
2024** (Jacobson & Carson).
*Why it rose:* incumbents got better at raising money and **scaring off quality
challengers** (Levitt & Wolfram; Cox & Katz) — in 2024 the average House
incumbent raised **more than 6×** the average challenger and the average Senate
incumbent **more than 11×**; plus the **personal vote**, built from visibility,
the **franking privilege**, and **casework**.
*Why it fell:* **party loyalty increased.** District partisan composition now
predicts outcomes; it has become very hard to build a personal vote that pulls
opposing partisans. **Incumbents still win — on their district's partisan lean —
but earn few "extra votes" for being incumbents.**
**The "running scared" paradox** (Anthony King): incumbents behave as if
endangered because losses, though rare, are catastrophic and career-ending — so
they fundraise constantly to **signal invulnerability**.

### Quality of congressional elections
**Aggregate competitiveness is real; individual competitiveness is not.** Since
1990 House control has switched **five** times and Senate control **six**. But in
**2024 only 67 of 435 House races and 11 of 34 Senate races were decided by fewer
than 10 points** (Figure 10.3). Competitiveness fell from 1952 to the late 1980s
as the incumbency advantage grew, then spiked only when realities endangered many
incumbents at once (1992 bank scandal; 1994, 2010, 2018 waves).
Consequences: uncompetitive races fail **free choice** and **deliberation** —
less spending, less media, and **voters in less competitive districts know less
about their representatives** (Lipsitz) — and weaken **accountability**.
**But the trade-offs are stated fairly:** competitive districts leave many voters
on the losing side, **less trusting of and satisfied with their representative**
(Brunell & Buchler), and cost more, magnifying money concerns.
**Primaries as the substitute accountability mechanism** — which is exactly why
direct primaries were adopted — do provide some (Hirano & Snyder), **but only
48% of Democratic and Republican House primaries were contested in 2024**.
**Nationalization evaluated both ways:** it gives voters simple, usable
information (party labels and familiar issues) — *good* for deliberation in one
sense; but it strips out local information (community economic development,
effects of federal cuts on a local university) and **pressures potential
candidates to be loyal partisans, dissuading those who don't fit a party and
shrinking voters' choices**.

**Conclusion:** Mayhew's "single-minded seekers of reelection"; the two-year cycle
makes House members permanent campaigners. Lee's insight — **when majority
control is always in play, the minority has no incentive to hand the majority
legislative victories**, so the permanent campaign produces gridlock. The
closing trade-off is elegant and debate-ready: **the permanent campaign may
improve representation even as it degrades governance.**

**Key terms:** incumbent, open-seat, quality challengers, political amateurs,
earned/free media, coordinated expenditures, independent expenditures,
incumbency advantage, personal vote, franking privilege, casework.

**For Discussion:**
1. What kinds of people make successful congressional candidates?
2. What roles do parties play in congressional campaigns?
3. What explains the declining incumbency advantage?
4. How has the growing strength of partisan voting affected congressional elections?

> **Course notes.**
> - **This is the Week 11 debate, confirmed and sharpened.** The claim is
>   quantified: incumbency advantage **~10 points (1986) → 1.2 points (2024)**,
>   alongside **97%/88% reelection rates**. The apparent contradiction is the
>   whole lesson, and the election-night lab's 433-district baseline can test it
>   directly — compute district partisan lean vs. incumbent vote share and see
>   how little is left over.
> - **67 of 435 House races within 10 points** is the single cleanest
>   competitiveness statistic in the book and belongs in the same lab.
> - The **media-market vs. district mismatch** (New Jersey especially) is a
>   lovely geography-meets-data point and would extend the Week 6 media-market
>   material naturally.
> - Q3 is the debate prompt; Q4 is the better essay prompt.
> - **48% of House primaries contested (2024)** matches Ch. 1's figure for
>   primaries with ≥2 candidates — the book uses the same Ballotpedia source in
>   both places, so the numbers are consistent and can be cited either way.

---

## Chapter 11 — State and Local Campaigns

Two openers, deliberately at opposite ends of the spectrum.
**Oklahoma, Nov 5 2024:** three supreme court justices faced retention after
crossing Gov. Kevin Stitt (religious charter school funding, school mask rules).
Judges may not campaign; **PACs face no such limit**, so **~$3.5 million in dark
money** flooded a retention race. **Yvonne Kauger lost by 7,047 votes out of 1.4
million — the first Oklahoma justice ever to lose a retention election.**
**Oceanside, California, the same night:** the mayoral race took **more than two
weeks** to count; the challenger's 591-vote lead shrank to 31 and then vanished.
**Final margin: 291 votes out of 78,000.**

**Scale, which students never appreciate:** 50 states, **3,144 counties**,
**13,187 school districts**, **100,000+ cities and towns**; plus initiatives,
referenda, and recalls — **383 officials targeted by recall efforts in 2024**,
mostly city council and school board members. These are also the offices that
**decided pandemic closures, mask mandates, and stay-at-home orders**.
**Roll-off (undervoting)** is introduced here: voters stop marking as they move
down the ballot, in part because civics teaches the presidency and Congress and
never the secretary of state, public advocate, or coroner.

**The chapter's thesis, stated bluntly and worth quoting in the syllabus:**
> *"State and local elections—the ones that arguably affect citizens' daily lives
> the most—are often the ones that live up to ideals of political campaigns the
> least."*

### Three local rules that change everything
**1. Nonpartisan ballots.** A Progressive anti-machine reform. **Over 75% of city
council and mayoral elections are nonpartisan.** Effects (Schaffner/Streb/Wright):
**lower turnout and higher roll-off**, because removing the party cue raises the
cost of voting — and voters substitute **incumbency** as the cue instead, so
**nonpartisan ballots help incumbents win**.
**2. At-large vs. district (ward) elections.** **Under 20% of cities use districts
exclusively** (more common in the largest metros); **just over 20% use mixed
systems**; **the majority use at-large exclusively**, especially cities under
200,000. Mayors are always at-large. **At-large elections disadvantage candidates
of color where the electorate is mostly White** — used deliberately in Jim Crow
southern municipalities — and district systems help only if boundaries keep
communities intact rather than splitting them (Trounstine & Valdini; Abott &
Magazinnik). Honest caveat: if White voters will support candidates of color,
switching to districts won't diversify the council.
**3. Off-cycle elections — the biggest single finding in the chapter.**
More than half of cities and school districts vote off-cycle; some school
districts hold general elections in **April or May**. Four states hold statewide
elections in odd years (LA, MS, NJ, AL — and the later gubernatorial list is
KY, LA, MS, NJ, VA).
- **Turnout in off-cycle mayoral elections is over 20 points lower than
  presidential-concurrent, and more than 15 points lower than midterm-concurrent**
  (Marschall & Lappie).
- **Off-cycle elections *reduce* the incumbency advantage in mayoral races**
  (de Benedictis-Kessner) — the small electorate is more engaged and relies less
  on the incumbency cue. A genuinely counterintuitive result.

### The reality of small-scale democracy
Small jurisdictions have thin service portfolios (water, sewage, police, fire,
parks, streets) and therefore **few targetable benefits** compared with federal
officeholders.
**The news vacuum, quantified:** local races are **less than 5% of campaign-
related TV news stories**, even in midterms (Fowler et al.); consolidation of
local stations into national conglomerates made it worse (Martin & McCrain);
**1 in 5 U.S. newspapers closed between 2004 and 2018**; **half of counties have
a single newspaper and 5% are "news deserts"** (Abernathy).
**Local incumbency advantage exceeds the U.S. House's:** in mayoral elections
1988–2016, **incumbents were 33% more likely than nonincumbents to win** (Warshaw;
Trounstine).
**Nationalization has reached the local level** — presidential and local vote
correlation is rising, and partisanship now distorts even evaluations of whether
local education and crime policies are working (Dyck & Pearson-Merkowitz).

### State-level rules
**Term limits** — most states cap governors (usually two consecutive terms); a
substantial minority cap legislators. **But term limits do not reliably increase
competitiveness**: first-termers draw no serious challenger because good
challengers wait for the open seat, and party leaders then coordinate on one
favorite for that seat. Worse, **term-limited offices appear to attract
ideologically extreme candidates and contribute to polarization** (Myers).
**Legislative professionalism** — session length, pay, staff. **New Mexico
legislators receive no salary at all**, only expense reimbursement; North Dakota
and Texas meet a few months every other year. Staff range from **New York's 2,850
to Vermont's 72**. **Four citizen legislatures** (MT, ND, SD, WY); about **25%
are highly professionalized**. Two-edged effect: professional legislatures
**attract more challengers** (the job is worth having) but **incumbents are less
vulnerable** because they have resources, casework, and fundraising capacity — so
**reelection rates are higher** in professionalized legislatures.
**Party organizational strength** — **Republicans are generally stronger
organizationally than Democrats**; parties are stronger in urban, educated
communities (social capital to tap) and in competitive jurisdictions (where they
are needed).
**Coattail effect** — **each additional 10 points of gubernatorial vote share
brings 1–2 points to that party's other statewide candidates** (Meredith).
Matters most for challengers and open seats, and more in the **seven
straight-ticket states**.

### Competitiveness — the numbers that matter most for the course
- **Governors: ~85% reelected in the 2010s; over 95% since 2020.**
- **State legislative primaries 2010–2024: fewer than 18% contested.**
- **2024 state legislative general elections: 38% of major-party candidates faced
  no major-party opposition** (Figure 11.1 maps this by state; worst in WY, ND,
  OK, AR, TN, AL, MA at 61%+).
- **By contrast, only 4% of 2024 U.S. House and Senate seats were uncontested.**
  So **state legislatures are roughly ten times less contested than Congress** —
  that single comparison is the strongest argument for the course's attention to
  down-ballot data.
- Clean elections laws raise the likelihood an incumbent is challenged, **but the
  effect is small**; public funding alone does not fix uncompetitive legislatures.

**National forces at the state level:** split outcomes are rarer; gubernatorial
and presidential voting increasingly correlated; the president's party loses
state legislative seats when the president is unpopular — **2010: Democrats lost
six governorships and nearly 700 state legislative seats, including control of
21 chambers**. Governors are judged partly on the **national** economy, which
they cannot affect (King & Cohen).
**The Moskowitz media-market design appears again, and this is its best use:**
people in an **in-state** media market see more coverage of their governor and
are **4–5 points more likely to split their presidential and gubernatorial
tickets** than demographically similar people in an out-of-state market (e.g.
southern New Jersey inside the Philadelphia market).

### Ballot initiatives
**24 states + DC allow initiatives; 23 allow referenda.** **159 statewide measures
in 2024**, including: three states rejecting private-school/homeschool funding;
**nine states voting on abortion rights (six yes, three no)**; **eight
Republican-controlled legislatures putting up amendments barring noncitizen
voting in local elections**; **six states + DC voting on ranked-choice voting
(five states rejected it)**; and **eight states considering primary changes — all
kept their current systems**.
**School bonds are the sleeper topic:** **40 states require voter approval**, and
in 2024 **75% of 2,274 school bonds passed, raising $115 billion** — despite most
voters having no school-age children. Why? **Ballot language emphasizes benefits
over costs; where personal cost must be stated, support drops** (Lang et al.).
**Money:** the **2022 California sports-betting fight cost over $400 million** —
the most expensive initiative campaign ever — DraftKings/FanDuel vs. Native
American tribes, and **it failed**. Limits are essentially unavailable: the Court
holds that **initiatives, unlike candidates, cannot be corrupted**, so there is no
compelling interest to restrict spending (Hasen).
An entire professional industry now exists — drafting firms, signature-gathering
firms, media firms, pollsters, direct mail. A consultant's line worth quoting:
**"With ballot issues you build your own candidate."**
**Effects are real but bounded:** the Oregon randomized ballot-guide experiment
shifted voting **3–6 points**, enough to plausibly flip two of twelve measures
(Rogers & Middleton). Working against them: **status quo bias** plus **negativity
bias** — "no" arguments are inherently the status-quo arguments, so initiatives
routinely underperform their polling.

### Judicial elections
**Only eight states elect no state-level judges.** Three types: **partisan**,
**nonpartisan**, **retention**.
**Money:** state supreme court candidate fundraising went from **$9.0M in
1989–90 to $100M in 2021–22** (Figure 11.2, Brennan Center). In 2022 **interest
groups were nearly half** of the $100M. **The April 2023 Wisconsin Supreme Court
race alone saw $51 million** and flipped the court's majority.
**Why groups bother** — the best quote in the chapter, from an Ohio union
official: **"We figured out a long time ago that it's easier to elect seven
judges than to elect 132 legislators."** Conservatives pushed judicial elections
knowing the electorate is less liberal than the bar and legal academy
(Bonica & Sen). Tort reform is the recurring battleground.
**The corruption case study: *Caperton v. A. T. Massey Coal* (2009).**
Massey lost a $50M verdict; CEO Don Blankenship spent **$3 million** through
"And for the Sake of the Kids" to defeat Justice McGraw and elect Brent Benjamin;
three years later **Benjamin cast the swing vote in a 3–2 decision overturning
the $50M verdict**; the U.S. Supreme Court threw it out on due process grounds.
**Retention elections have been weaponized** — Oklahoma 2024, and **three Iowa
justices removed in 2010 after ruling for same-sex marriage** (only three other
Iowa judges had ever lost in 48 years). And judges know it: **judges in retention
systems are demonstrably sensitive to public opinion** (Canes-Wrone/Clark/Park).

### Accountability and representation — the argument the course should own
Low visibility means **activists, parties, and interest groups get more influence
per dollar down-ballot than they could ever get federally**, so officials
represent **activists better than voters**.
**The teacher-pay finding is the cleanest causal story in the chapter:**
in school districts with **off-cycle** elections, **teachers are paid more** —
because unions' members vote reliably in low-turnout elections while older voters
without school-age children pay little attention. And **off-cycle school boards
elect members whose views are further from their constituents'** (Hartney; Hartney
& Hayes; Anzia; Kogan/Lavertu/Peskowitz).
**Who this excludes:** local voters are **Whiter, wealthier, and more
conservative** than nonvoters (Schaffner/Rhodes/La Raja, *Hometown Inequality*).
In low-turnout elections **candidates of color are more likely to lose** mayoral
and council races, and **local spending priorities diverge from the preferences
of constituents of color** (Hajnal). Same for housing — affordable projects get
derailed by residents Whiter and wealthier than the electorate as a whole
(Einstein/Glick/Palmer, *Neighborhood Defenders*).
**The remedy the book actually endorses: move local elections on-cycle.**
Presidential-concurrent local elections produce **higher turnout and an electorate
more representative in race, class, ideology, and party**, which improves
representation for people of color and lower-income people (Hajnal/Kogan/Markarian).
**Accountability evidence is weak:** little evidence that voters sanction state
legislators for unpopular votes or ideological extremity, and it is worse where
media coverage is thin (Rogers).

### The paradox, and the honest trade-off
Closest government, least engagement. **State and local turnout has stagnated or
declined even as presidential turnout rose.** Uncompetitive and uncontested races
fail **free choice** and **deliberation**; off-cycle rules and nonpartisan ballots
make it worse; and dominant local parties actively **perpetuate low
competitiveness and low turnout** (Trounstine, *Political Monopolies*).
**But the fix has costs, and the book insists on them.** Expensive judicial
elections — including retention elections — **increase turnout and reduce
incumbent judges' advantages**, and may improve deliberation by telling voters
what judges believe. In a 2008 survey **large majorities said judicial candidates
should be allowed to attack opponents and state views on controversial issues**,
and a before/after study of Kentucky's 2006 judicial elections found **most
respondents did not come to see the courts as less legitimate** (Gibson).
Closing: *"There is no easy way to make state and local elections more engaging
to voters without dealing with potential downsides."*

**Key terms:** professionalism, citizen legislatures, coattail effect, ballot
initiatives, referenda, retention elections.

**For Discussion:**
1. What is legislative professionalism, and how does it affect incumbents' advantages?
2. How do state and local campaign strategies differ from federal ones?
3. Why do state and local races give parties and interest groups more opportunity
   to influence outcomes than federal races do?
4. Advantages and disadvantages of making state and local elections more competitive?

> **Course notes.**
> - **This closes the biggest gap in `textbook-alignment.md` §6 — off-cycle
>   elections — and does it better than an outside reading would.** The turnout
>   deltas (**−20 points vs. presidential, −15 vs. midterm**), the **teacher-pay
>   result**, and the **on-cycle remedy** together form a complete, self-contained
>   argument about turnout inequality. No supplemental reading needed; the
>   chapter *is* the reading. I'd still add one journalistic piece for texture.
> - **Q4 is the Week 12 debate**, and it is unusually well-balanced because §"The
>   Paradox" supplies real evidence on both sides (judicial elections raise
>   turnout and reduce incumbency advantage *and* raise corruption concerns).
> - **The 38% vs. 4% uncontested comparison** (state legislative vs.
>   congressional) is the single best justification for a state-legislative
>   contestation lab, and Figure 11.1 gives a state-by-state map to reproduce
>   from Ballotpedia data.
> - **School bonds** are an unexpectedly good lab: 2,274 measures, 75% pass rate,
>   $115B, and a documented framing effect when personal cost is disclosed.
> - The **Oklahoma retention race** ($3.5M dark money, 7,047-vote margin) and
>   **Oceanside** (291 votes of 78,000, two weeks to count) are both excellent
>   first-day hooks for the state-and-local week.

