## Chapter 12 — Voter Participation

Opens with **one of the authors' own anecdote** — a conversation with a Cruz
operative days before the 2018 Texas Senate race. O'Rourke had visited **all 254
counties** and raised **$80 million**. Asked whether high turnout worried him,
the operative said the opposite: **the Cruz team *wanted* turnout above 8
million**, because O'Rourke's voters were certain to show up and the open
question was whether exurban and rural Republicans would. **8.4 million voted;
Cruz won by 2.6 points.**
Three lessons the chapter builds on: turnout is up and **negative partisanship is
part of why**; **"high turnout helps Democrats" is false**; and **lowering the
cost of voting rarely produces big turnout gains** — Texas turnout rose 18 points
from 2014 to 2018 "not because it was easier for Texans to vote but because they
thought the 2018 election mattered more."
The mechanism for the turnout myth (Shaw & Petrocik, *The Turnout Myth*):
**marginal voters are less interested and less informed, so they "blow with the
political wind"** — meaning high turnout can help *either* party depending on
conditions.

### Trends
**Presidential turnout: 67% (2020) → 64% (2024)**, against ~60% in the 1950s–60s,
a decline from 1972 to 2000 (1992 excepted, thanks to Perot), and a rebound from
2004. **Midterms ~40%, 10–20 points below presidential** — but **nearly 50% in
2018 and 47% in 2022**.
**The non-voting participation story is the more interesting one.** Figure 12.1
shows that while voting and **donating** stayed high, **attending meetings and
persuading others declined** — advocacy peaked at 50% in 2016 and fell to about
**36% in 2024**. The book's hypothesis is sharp: rising partisan hostility raises
participation up to a point, but **when hostility gets high enough people stop
wanting to interact**, so the forms of participation that require talking to
others decay while the solitary ones (voting, writing a check) hold up.
Midterms did *not* show the 1960–96 dip because midterm voters are **habitual
voters**; the presidential dip is attributed to **uncompetitive elections and
reduced GOTV investment** (Green & Smith).

### Comparative
**U.S. ranks 22nd of 64 countries holding presidential elections 2010–2020**; the
**2022 midterm would rank 68th of 100** parliamentary elections. Figure 12.3's
top four are all South American (Uruguay, Ecuador, Bolivia, Peru).
**But Americans are not less engaged** — they report *stronger* party attachment
and are *more* likely to believe voting matters. **It's the rules:**
- **No compulsory voting** (27 countries have it; enforced compulsory voting adds
  an estimated **15–19 points**).
- **Too many elections.** Countries that ask citizens to vote more often have
  lower turnout — duty is discharged at one level, and parties spread finite
  resources thin.
- **Registration is a separate step** everywhere but North Dakota, while Canada
  and others register automatically at 18 or naturalization and keep people on
  the rolls through moves.
- **"Use it or lose it" purges** cut the other way — removal for non-voting
  unless a mailed postcard is returned, and "such notices are easily missed."

### The ability / motivation / opportunity framework
**Ability — education and income.** Education is among the strongest predictors,
especially for time-intensive, social forms of participation. But the *mechanism*
is contested: recent work suggests **more civics courses matter less than
cultivating general student interest and responsibility** (Weinschenk & Dawes),
and college raises turnout **without it being clear that political coursework is
why**. Civic skills also come from workplaces and churches — **Black churches as
the organizational backbone of the civil rights movement** is the book's example.
Income matters less than education except for **donating**, where it is the
strongest predictor. The nice finding: **it isn't free time** — high-income
careers have little of it. Rather, **low-income people must work harder simply to
get by**, and participation rises with income only up to a comfortable standard
of living, then flattens.
**Motivation, individual:** **political interest** (the single most important
individual factor; stable across life; roots in parents, education, personality
openness, possibly genes); **political knowledge** (on par with education; strong
for voting and discussion, **no effect on donating**; men outscore women, but the
gap shrinks when questions cover topics more relevant to women);
**partisan strength** (rises with age, tied to extroversion); and **self-interest**
via **material**, **solidary**, and **purposive benefits**.
**Negative partisanship motivates turnout but less than positive partisanship
does** (Bankert) — a useful corrective to the usual story.

### Mobilization — and Table 12.1
**Message content barely matters; contact method matters enormously.** Civic duty
appeals are no better or worse than "the election will be close."
**TV advertising does not mobilize** — voters in ad-saturated markets are no more
likely to vote than those in markets with none (Krasno & Green), and **negative
advertising does not consistently depress turnout** (Lau/Sigelman/Rovner
meta-analysis).

**Table 12.1 — Effectiveness and cost per vote** (Green & Gerber, *Get Out the
Vote*, 5th ed., 2024, 172–73) — the `gotv/` lab's source table:

| Rank | Activity | Effectiveness | Approx. cost per vote |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Door-to-door canvassing | 1 per 17 contacted | $57 |
| 2 | Phone calls by volunteers | 1 per 36 contacted | $46 |
| 3 | Phone calls by telemarketers | 1 per 106 contacted | $106 |
| 4 | Nonpartisan direct mail | 1 per 260 reached | $130 |
| 5 | Robocalls | 1 per 425 | $64 |
| 6 | Direct mail from campaigns | No significant effect | — |
| 7 | Emails | No significant effect | — |
| 8 | Leaving materials on doors | No significant effect | — |

> ⚠️ **Two internal inconsistencies in this section — both usable in the lab.**
> 1. **Prose vs. table on telemarketers.** The text says "Phone calls made by
>    telemarketers and text messaging are far less effective (one per 260 and 381,
>    respectively)" — but **Table 12.1 gives telemarketers as 1 per 106**. The
>    prose figure looks like it was copied from the nonpartisan-mail row (also
>    260). Also note **text messaging (1 per 381) appears only in the prose, not
>    in the table**.
> 2. **Robocalls.** The table's note says cost per vote "is not calculated for
>    tactics that are not proven to raise turnout," and the prose lists robocalls
>    among tactics with **no significant effect** — yet the table gives robocalls
>    both an effectiveness figure (1 per 425) **and** a $64 cost per vote.
> Students can catch both by reading the table against the paragraph. This is the
> same skill the Table 5.2/5.3 erratum exercises.

**The cost-effectiveness inversion worth teaching:** canvassing is the *most
effective* but **volunteer phone calls are cheaper per vote ($46 vs. $57)**
because canvassing is labor-intensive. Effectiveness and efficiency rank
differently — exactly the tension a $250k budget-constraint exercise exposes.
**Messenger matters:** campaign direct mail doesn't work; **nonpartisan and
governmental mail does**. A Facebook GOTV experiment among the author's
own friended students **raised turnout 8 points** (Teresi & Michelson).
**The uncomfortable conclusion:** mobilization is easier among those already
likely to vote, so **GOTV may actually widen participatory inequality**
(Enos/Fowler/Vavreck; Arceneaux & Nickerson).

### Social networks
**Voting is contagious.** Household members of a GOTV contact are significantly
more likely to vote, especially the young (Nickerson) — the **spillover effect**.
The **61-million-person Facebook experiment** (Bond et al.) again. And **social
pressure works**: threatening to reveal voting history to neighbors or publish
voter lists is effective (Gerber/Green/Larimer).
**Family and school:** dinner-table political conversation transmits interest;
**student government, community service, debate, and drama predict adult
participation — sports do not**, and the effect holds **regardless of family
income or parental education**. The policy implication the book draws: **cutting
these activities may reduce future participation.**
**Context of community:** turnout gaps between White people and communities of
color are **smaller where those communities are a larger share of the county**
(Fraga); partisans living in "enemy territory" participate and discuss less; young
people in older areas vote less.
**Generational cohorts:** the Greatest Generation as the high-water mark, then
decline through Boomers, X, Millennials, Z. Putnam's estimate: **each additional
hour of daily TV is associated with a 10% reduction in civic activism** — and
**most young people now spend 3+ hours a day on screens** (CDC).

### Opportunity
Excluded populations: **74 million children, 13 million permanent residents,
4 million people with felony convictions**.
Registration is a **Progressive-era anti-machine invention**, not a neutral
administrative step — a point the chapter makes explicitly and which pairs with
Ch. 3's turnout collapse. **No state now closes registration more than 31 days
out.** **Motor Voter (NVRA, 1993)** did not significantly raise turnout, but
**same-day and automatic registration do, modestly**.
**Casting a ballot:** ~85% of registered people vote in presidential elections.
Since 2020, **Michigan, South Carolina, and Texas reduced polling places**;
consolidation and relocation reduce turnout through confusion (Yoder), though
**mailing sample ballots or polling-place information counteracts it**.
Convenience voting recap: **absentee and vote-by-mail +2–3 points** (mail's
benefit decays); **early voting no clear gain**, possibly because it **makes
systematic mobilization harder**.
**California's Voter's Choice Act (2016)** is the best positive case: mail ballots
to all, drop boxes, vote centers. **In 2018 the first five VCA counties gained
~5 points of turnout vs. ~2 points in non-VCA counties.**
**Competitiveness creates opportunity — and the mechanism is not what students
assume.** Competitiveness raises participation **not because voters think their
vote is more likely to matter, but because they are more likely to be asked**
(Gerber et al., "One in a Million").

### Group differences
**Methodological warning the book puts up front, and it is important for any lab
using self-reports:** Figure 12.4 is **self-reported**. Over-reporting is
concentrated among **older, educated, wealthy, politically engaged** people, so
**income and age gaps are very likely exaggerated**. **Race is unrelated to
over-reporting; men over-report significantly more than women** (Ansolabehere &
Hersh).
- **Income:** 2024 reported turnout **71% (>$75k) vs. 46% (<$20k) — a ~25-point
  gap**. The book notes the puzzle (the dissatisfied participate less) and
  resolves it through education, civic skills, networks, and reachability.
- **Age:** 2024 **48% (18–24) vs. 75% (65+)**. Young people **intend to vote as
  much as older people but don't follow through** (Holbein & Hillygus) — mobility
  forces re-registration and makes them hard to target; life transitions
  (especially **leaving school and getting married**) depress turnout; aging has
  an independent positive effect, probably through learning.
- **Race:** VRA closed Black–White *registration* gaps in MS, NC, AL from **50+
  points to under 10 within 20 years**. **2008: parity for the first time. 2012:
  Black turnout exceeded White (66% vs. 62%). 2016: White +4 (63 vs. 59).
  2020: White +8 (71 vs. 63). 2024: the gap grew again.**
  Latino and Asian American turnout is lower — for Latinos explained by
  **education and income, not ethnicity**; for Asian Americans by **nativity and
  national origin** (Indian and Japanese Americans highest in 2024).
  Black turnout is described as **remarkable given lower average income and
  education**, credited to church mobilization, sustained Democratic courting, and
  more recently **Black Lives Matter protest activity** (Gillion).
- **Gender:** 2024 **67% of women vs. 64% of men**; the gap is widest among the
  unmarried (**56% vs. 50%**). Few gaps in meetings or campaign work, but men
  **donate more, discuss more, and run more**.
  The role-model finding: **when women hold high-profile office, young women and
  girls become more interested in politics** (Campbell & Wolbrecht) — hence a
  vicious cycle sustaining underrepresentation.
- **Race × gender — the section that matters most for our Week 14 material.**
  The book is candid that **scholars usually study race *or* gender, rarely the
  intersection**. Black women are the exception studied: they vote at much higher
  rates than Black men and participate more than White women in other ways —
  **in 2020, Black women were 8 points more likely to vote than Black men and
  voted at a rate similar to White women** — and this **is not explained by the
  resource model**. The explanations offered are **linked fate** (Brown) and
  **mobilization in areas with high concentrations of African Americans**
  (Stauffer & Fraga).

> ⚠️ **Suspected erratum in the race paragraph.** The text states the 2024
> White–Black gap "grew to almost 11 percent (80 versus 71 percent)." Two
> problems: **80 − 71 = 9, not "almost 11,"** and an 80% White self-reported rate
> is irreconcilable with the **63.5% overall** self-reported rate in Figure 12.4
> (and with 2020's 71 vs. 63). Verify against the Census "Voting and Registration
> in the Election of November 2024" table before using these numbers in a lab.

### Are voters representative of nonvoters?
Two questions: would different candidates win, and would different policies
follow?
**Candidates: mostly no.** Turnout increases don't consistently favor either
party. Nonvoters lean slightly Democratic on average, **but the lean varies by
state and year, and few elections are close enough for it to matter**
(Citrin/Schickler/Sides).
**Policy: it depends on the issue.** Wolfinger and Rosenstone's classic line —
voters are "virtually a carbon copy of the citizen population" — is now qualified.
Figure 12.5 (CES 2020, **validated** voting): small differences on guns,
abortion, immigration, policing; **large ones on health care and military use**.
**73% of nonvoters vs. 58% of voters support expanding Medicare to cover
everyone.** On military intervention, **~51–52% of voters vs. ~41–43% of
nonvoters** support intervening in a genocide or helping the UN uphold
international law. The explanations offered — nonvoters are less likely to be
insured, and are younger, less educated, and more racially diverse, i.e. **more
likely to serve or know someone who serves**.

**Conclusion** raises the trade-off honestly: participatory inequality matters,
**but** turnout changes would alter only some outcomes because so many elections
are uncompetitive (citing the NYT's "The Death of Competition in American
Elections"), while **policy does appear more responsive to participants than
non-participants** (Dassonneville et al.). And the counter-argument is given its
due: some hold that **informed voting matters more than universal voting**.

**Key terms:** electoral participation, participatory inequalities, GOTV efforts,
political interest, material/solidary/purposive benefits, mobilization, social
network, generational cohorts, Motor Voter Act.

**For Discussion:**
1. How much does it matter if some groups participate more than others?
2. How can voting be made more convenient for young people?
3. Are you interested in politics? Who or what encouraged it?
4. Do you benefit from participating? How?
5. What new mobilization techniques will candidates use in future elections?

> **Course notes.**
> - **Table 12.1 is the `gotv/` lab, confirmed**, and it is better than expected
>   because of the **effectiveness/efficiency inversion** (canvassing most
>   effective, volunteer calls cheapest) and the **two internal inconsistencies**
>   above, which give students something real to catch.
> - **The race × gender section is the direct bridge to the Week 14
>   BISG/surnames material** — and the book's own admission that intersectional
>   participation is under-studied is the strongest possible motivation for that
>   lab. The **linked fate** and **community concentration** findings are the
>   hypotheses to test.
> - **The self-report warning (Ansolabehere & Hersh) should be taught before any
>   survey-based turnout lab.** That over-reporting is concentrated among the
>   already-advantaged, and that **men over-report more than women while race is
>   unrelated**, is exactly the kind of measurement lesson this course exists for.
> - Q1 is the Week 10 debate; the voters-vs-nonvoters figure gives it evidence
>   rather than intuition.
> - **This chapter confirms `textbook-alignment.md` §4(d): Ch. 12 is far richer
>   than "turnout."** It is really three chapters — comparative rules, individual
>   psychology, and group inequality — and it may deserve two sessions rather
>   than one.

---

## Chapter 13 — Voter Choice

Opens on the **competing post-mortems of 2024** and refuses to pick a side
cheaply. The critique: Harris never separated from Biden — the *View* answer,
**"Not a thing comes to mind,"** as the crystallizing moment, compounded by her
2019 positions. The campaign's defense: 107 days, brutal economic headwinds, and
a deliberate two-prong plan (rebrand her as a "middle-class girl" prosecutor
against a "convicted felon"; make the race a **choice**, not a **referendum**).
**The evidence that adjudicates it:** 39% of voters named the economy/jobs as
most important, and **61% of them voted Trump**. But the chapter's real move is
the forecasts — **political science models published in August 2024, before the
fall campaign, averaged Trump at 50.2% of the two-party vote and 292 electoral
votes. He got 50.8% and 312.** (Honest caveats: of 12 models, **three predicted
Harris and five predicted Trump** in the electoral college; the models badly
missed 2000, predicting Gore at ~56% when he got just over 50%; and poll-based
2016 analyses gave Clinton **70–99%**.)
**The question this poses is the course's own question:** *"If the campaign
matters, how can presidential elections be forecast with such accuracy so far in
advance?"*

### Five inputs to vote choice
**Baseline fact:** in every presidential election **1952–2024, fewer than half**
of Americans said they were "very interested in the current campaign" — though
Figure 13.1 shows "very interested" at a **series high of 48% in 2024**, up from
a 26% low in 1996, with "not much interested" down to **16%**.

**1. Party identification** — a *psychological attachment*, distinct from
ideology, registration, and vote (though correlated with all three). Michigan
school, *The American Voter* (1960). Learned early, mostly from parents;
**cohort effects** matter (people coming of age under a popular president adopt
that party — Reagan vs. Nixon cohorts as the example); durable thereafter.
**The independents question, answered with the seven-point scale:** self-described
independents rose from **5% (1952) to 15% (2024)**, but **only about 7% are
"pure" independents** who lean toward neither party, a figure **stable since the
mid-1980s** — and leaners vote almost like partisans (Petrocik).
Three reasons it matters: it is a **filter** on information (hence partisans
always think their candidate won the debate); it **motivates turnout**; and it
**predicts vote** — **over 90% of Democrats and Republicans vote for their own
nominee** (Figure 13.3, where both parties sat near 90% from 2000 on, after
Perot-driven dips to 80%/70% in 1992).
**Negative / affective partisanship** (Figure 13.4): partisans' ratings of *their
own* party have held steady in the 60–80 range (2024: D 70, R 78), while
**ratings of the opposing party fell from the 40s in the 1980s into the teens by
2024**. That divergence — own-party flat, other-party collapsing — is the single
best chart in the book for teaching what polarization actually is.
**Defection is now rare:** per AP VoteCast, **only 5% split presidential and
congressional ballots in 2024** (3% Harris + Republican, 2% Trump + Democrat).
Still, 2024 produced **four Trump states electing Democratic senators (AZ, MI, NV,
WI)** and **New Hampshire voting Harris and electing Republican Kelly Ayotte
governor**.

**2. Social groups** — Columbia school (Lazarsfeld; Berelson). Groups acquire
party attachments through historical experience and party attention; then
partisanship becomes the proximate cause, but **the group connection is why the
partisanship exists**.
Magnitudes: **~90% of Black voters have backed Democratic presidential candidates
in every election since the VRA; 83% voted Harris in 2024. 79% of White
born-again/evangelical Christians voted Trump.** Figure 13.5 (Fox News Voter
Analysis, **N = 119,967**) gives the full 2024 breakdown.
**The mechanism is conversation, not demography:** voting one's group identity
depends on **regular contact with other group members** and on **opinion
leaders** who supply the "people like you should vote for this candidate"
reminder. The 1948 example — Catholic-church and union opinion leaders framing
Truman as for the "little guy" against Dewey the "business-friendly fat cat."

**3. Incumbent performance** — **retrospective voting** ("are we better off than
when the incumbent took office?"), **sociotropic** (national) rather than
**pocketbook** (personal), and weighted toward **the election year itself**, so
three bad years plus one good one can still be rewarded.
**But partisanship contaminates the input.** The book's newest and best example:
in **October 2024 Democrats rated the economy positive by 2 points (51/49);
by January 2025, right after Trump's inauguration, negative by 13 (43/56)** —
same economy. And in 2016, **57% of Democrats called the economy "bad" or "very
bad" under a Democratic administration, and 72% of them voted for Clinton
anyway.** Conclusion: **it is independents whose vote actually responds to
economic performance.**

**4. Policy issues — and the answer is mostly no.** **Proximity voting** requires
having opinions, knowing candidates' positions, and computing distance; voters
rarely do all three, and candidates are deliberately ambiguous. **One prominent
study estimates fewer than half the electorate has both the opinions and the
candidate knowledge required for issue voting.** Where it happens it is confined
to **issue publics** (seniors on Medicare being the model case). And the
reverse-causation finding matters: **voters more often adopt the positions of the
candidate they already support** than choose a candidate by position (Lenz).

**5. Candidate traits.**
*Gender:* voters say they will vote for women, but the traits associated with
political leadership — competence, ambition, aggressiveness, confidence,
toughness — are coded masculine, so **men are assumed viable while women must
work to be taken seriously** (Ditonto et al.), and **likability trades off against
the strength women need to overcome the stereotype** — Klobuchar, Harris, Palin,
Clinton cited as walking that tightrope.
*Race:* White opposition to Black candidates can come from overt racism **or**
from stereotypes — **White respondents perceive Black candidates as less
competent and more liberal**. But newer work finds **White voters more aware of
racial stereotypes may be more receptive to Black candidates** (Highton).
**The caveat that governs both:** partisanship "typically overwhelms the
significance of candidates' demographic characteristics. Candidates matter—but
only for a relatively small subset of the electorate."
*Appearance and personality:* better-looking candidates do get more votes, and
positive trait assessments predict vote — **but the causal identification problem
is stated plainly**: attractive candidates **select into winnable races**
(Atkinson/Enos/Hill), so the face–vote correlation may be selection, not effect.
Traits matter most **where information is scarce** — small races, unfamiliar
candidates. **Trait ownership** (Hayes): Democrats "own" empathy, Republicans
"own" leadership, and candidates who **counter-signal** (a Democrat projecting
strength, a Republican projecting caring) do better with independents and
crossover partisans.

### When campaigns matter — the two conditions
**(1) When candidates are unfamiliar. (2) When one side's resources dominate.**
Where resources are matched, "the respective efforts of the two sides thus tend
to cancel each other out."

**Presidential primaries: campaigns matter most.** Resources are wildly unequal,
candidates are unknown, and **party ID is useless because everyone shares it**.
The **2020 Biden–Sanders swing is the worked example**: Biden 28% / Sanders 19%
at the start; after Nevada (Feb 22) **Biden 17% / Sanders 29%**; after South
Carolina (Feb 29), **by March 11 — 17 days later — Biden 53% / Sanders 35%.**
The book notes this swing is "much larger than the swings typically seen in
presidential general election campaigns."

**Presidential general elections: campaigns matter least.** Candidates are known
(five or six years for an incumbent), and resources are effectively equal — not
just money but **motivation, information, and expertise** (Stuart Stevens's "the
big enchilada"). Trump was outspent in 2016, 2020, *and* 2024, with outside
groups closing gaps. Hence: **fight to a draw, and fundamentals decide.**
But the exceptions are documented: Biden outspending Trump in AZ/PA/MI/WI in the
last two months of 2020; **Bush outspending Gore late in 2000, possibly worth
four points of margin**; and **Obama 2008's spending edges — Florida $28M,
Virginia $16.3M, Indiana $11.4M, North Carolina $7.2M — in four states that had
gone Republican in 2004 by 5, 8, 21, and 12 points and all flipped.**
**The headline number: of 14 presidential elections 1948–2000, only five appear
to have been decided by the campaign** (Campbell).

**Non-presidential elections: campaigns matter more.** Voters don't know the
candidates, and resource disparities are large. **In congressional elections
2000–2024, no challenger who raised under $100,000 won; roughly one-third of
those who raised at least $1 million did.** Spending matters even more in state
legislative, state supreme court, and city council races, where **voters will see
nothing about a challenger unless the challenger's campaign produces it**.

### How campaigns matter — three mechanisms
**Persuasion.** Little is known about what messaging works; consultants rely on
polls, focus groups, and guesswork. On **tone**: negative advertising is **more
memorable** (negativity stands out — the arguing-couple-in-a-restaurant analogy),
and it does lower views of the target — **but it also lowers views of the
attacker, and the backlash may be larger**. "Negative advertising is thus a risky
strategy at best."
**Wedge issues** exploit **cross pressures** — the 2016 marijuana-initiative
strategy aimed at libertarian Republicans is the case — but with ~90% party
loyalty, base-solidifying usually beats wedging.
**Timing:** effects **decay fast**. Perry's 2006 Texas ads moved the polls and the
effect **faded within a week**; similar decay found in 2006, 2012, 2016. But the
book cautions against concluding early campaigning is useless — images and issue
priorities set early, and **with so many voters casting ballots before Election
Day, an October blitz may be too late**.
**Reinforcement.** Solidifying existing preferences — "rallying the base."
**Underappreciated because it is hard to measure**: a horse-race poll captures
direction but not certainty, so the campaign-long conversion of soft leaners into
committed voters is invisible in the toplines.
**Priming.** The mechanism that **reconciles predictable outcomes with volatile
polls** — the chapter's best analytic payoff. As campaigns proceed they focus
attention on fundamentals, so **polls converge toward the forecast**
(Gelman & King). And it is strategic: **candidates advantaged by conditions who
actually campaign on those conditions win more often than similarly situated
candidates who campaign on something else** (Vavreck, *The Message Matters*).
2024 as the illustration: Trump priming the economy; Harris priming Trump himself.

### Conclusion — "is the process broken?"
On **deliberation**, the chapter turns the standard on citizens: if media and
candidates supply information and voters aren't paying attention, **voters bear
responsibility too**. But it then defends the shortcuts: the test of a good
heuristic is **whether it delivers the same choice full information would have**,
and for party ID and social identity the answer is often yes.
On **free choice**, fears of manipulation by clever advertising are judged
"by and large… unfounded": campaigns reinforce, occasionally persuade, and
**stop well short of manipulation**, because reality constrains them and because
each side offsets the other.
**But the margins are the caveat:** **Trump's 2024 win rested on under 230,000
votes across Michigan, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin; Biden's 2020 win on under
43,000 across Arizona, Georgia, and Wisconsin.** So campaigns can decide
elections even if they usually don't.
**The book's closing verdict on whether American elections are broken is
explicit and worth assigning directly:** 2024's result was accepted, feared
violence didn't materialize, candidates campaigned on common themes, and party ID
predicted the vote. *"When things go awry, the fault most often lies with
political leaders who sow the seeds of distrust. The solution is not to change
the electoral system but for political leaders to graciously accept defeat…
After all, in a democracy, there is always the next election."*

**Key terms:** party identification, negative partisanship, affective
partisanship, heuristic, social groups, opinion leaders, retrospective voting,
sociotropic voting, pocketbook voting, proximity voting, persuasion, wedge
issues, cross pressures, reinforcement, priming.

**For Discussion:**
1. What is party identification, and will negative partisanship keep growing?
2. Why do scholars think most voters don't engage in issue voting?
3. Describe three ways campaigns might "matter."
4. Why are political scientists skeptical that presidential campaigns decide outcomes?
5. Does the 2024 election confirm or contradict this chapter's findings?

> **Course notes.**
> - **Q5 is the best final-week prompt in the entire book** — it asks students to
>   adjudicate the book against the election it was written about, using the
>   August forecast (50.2% predicted / 50.8% actual) against the 230,000-vote
>   margin. Both facts are in the chapter and they point opposite ways.
> - **Figure 13.4 is the polarization chart to use all term** — own-party ratings
>   flat, other-party ratings collapsing from the 40s to the teens. It shows
>   polarization is asymmetric in a way "the parties moved apart" does not.
> - The **October 2024 → January 2025 economic-perception flip among Democrats**
>   pairs with Ch. 6's one-week post-election flip among Republicans. **Two
>   independent demonstrations of the same mechanism, in opposite directions** —
>   together they are a complete lesson on motivated reasoning.
> - **The forecast-vs-campaign tension is the course's thesis in miniature** and
>   belongs in Week 1 as well as the last week.

