# Synthesis — what this read changes

## 1. Errata and internal inconsistencies found (all verified against the text)

| Where | Problem |
|---|---|
| **Table 5.3, statewide row** | 2016 Republican total given as **2,289,880**; Table 5.2 and the NC State Board of Elections say **2,298,880**. Transposed digits. County rows are consistent — the error is confined to the statewide row. *(Confirms the note already in `textbook-alignment.md` §8.)* |
| **Ch. 12 prose vs. Table 12.1** | Prose says telemarketer calls are "one per 260"; **Table 12.1 says 1 per 106**. The prose figure appears copied from the nonpartisan-mail row. Text messaging (1 per 381) appears **only in the prose**, not the table. |
| **Table 12.1, robocalls** | Listed in the prose among tactics with **no significant effect**, and the table's note says cost is not calculated for such tactics — yet the row carries both an effectiveness figure (1 per 425) **and** a $64 cost per vote. |
| **Ch. 12, race turnout** | "the gap grew to almost 11 percent (80 versus 71 percent)" — **80 − 71 = 9**, and an 80% White self-reported rate cannot be squared with Figure 12.4's **63.5% overall**. Verify against the Census November 2024 table before using. |
| **Ch. 9, ad spending scope** | **$356M** (campaigns, Aug 8–Nov 5) vs. **$461M/$447M** (campaigns + parties) are different universes presented close together without flagging it. Not an error, but a trap. |

Three of these are genuinely useful teaching material: catching them requires
only reading a table against its own paragraph.

## 2. Status of the four supplemental-reading gaps in `textbook-alignment.md` §6

- **Ch. 3, era boundaries — partly self-solving.** "The Case for Continuity"
  argues against the book's own five-era scheme. The contestable boundaries are
  **1896** and especially **2016**. Still worth one outside historical piece.
- **Ch. 7, interest groups / ballot initiatives — CLOSED.** The chapter supplies
  Lupia's endorsement-shortcut result, the defeat-vs-pass asymmetry, the
  six-Californias stunt, the post-*Dobbs* wave, and the LA school board case.
- **Ch. 11, off-cycle elections — CLOSED, and it was the biggest gap.** Turnout
  deltas (−20 vs. presidential, −15 vs. midterm), the reduced incumbency
  advantage off-cycle, the teacher-pay finding, and the on-cycle remedy form a
  complete argument. One journalistic piece for texture would still help.
- **Ch. 8, algorithms — CLOSED, and it inverts the expected finding.** Facebook
  and YouTube experiments show algorithms mostly **don't** radicalize; users
  self-select. That is a better discussion than the one I was planning.

**Net: three of four gaps close from the text itself.** The remaining need is
Ch. 3 era boundaries.

## 3. New lab and data opportunities not previously in our materials

- **Figure 9.3 forecasting** — one predictor (Q3-to-Q3 real disposable income),
  a published 2024 prediction of "just over 50%," an actual of 49.2%.
- **Ch. 10 competitiveness** — 67 of 435 House races within 10 points; incumbency
  advantage ~10 points (1986) → **1.2 points (2024)** against **97%/88%**
  reelection. The paradox is directly testable in the Week 11 election-night lab.
- **Ch. 11 contestation** — **38% of 2024 state legislative candidates unopposed
  vs. 4% of congressional seats**, mappable by state (Figure 11.1).
- **School bonds** — 2,274 measures, 75% pass, $115B, plus a documented framing
  effect when personal cost is disclosed.
- **Ch. 13 margins** — 2024 decided by <230,000 votes across MI/PA/WI; 2020 by
  <43,000 across AZ/GA/WI.

## 4. Structural observations for the schedule

- **The book's own four standards (Table 1.1) are applied explicitly in the
  closing section of most chapters** — Ch. 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11, 12, 13 all have
  one. The debates should follow the book's application, not a parallel scheme.
- **Ch. 12 is really three chapters** (comparative rules / individual psychology /
  group inequality) and may deserve two sessions.
- **Nationalization is the book's true through-line**, not "rules, reality,
  strategy, choice." It appears substantively in Chs. 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, 11,
  and 13. If the syllabus names one organizing idea beyond the four-part
  framework, this is the one that actually recurs.
- **The measurement lessons are unusually good and are scattered**:
  Ansolabehere–Hersh on turnout over-reporting (Ch. 12), Lauderdale–Rivers on
  convention-bounce response bias (Ch. 9), post-2016 Republican survey
  non-response (Ch. 5), poll coverage and subgroup margins of error (Ch. 8),
  and the attractive-candidates selection problem (Ch. 13). **Collected, these
  are a complete "how measurement misleads" unit** and they are the most
  course-aligned material in the book.
